QB.add(10,[{"n":1,"q":"Which of the following is seen in Subclinical acute myocarditis?","o":[{"t":"Palpitations"},{"t":"Elevated pro BNP, Trop T"},{"t":"Low LVEF on 2D ECHO"},{"t":"Elevated creatinine levels"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Elevated pro-BNP, Trop T:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myocarditis encompasses a spectrum of immune-mediated processes affecting the heart, characterized by lymphocyte and macrophage involvement, and antibody-mediated injury. This can lead to structural and functional abnormalities in cardiomyocytes, resulting in contractile impairment, chamber stiffness, or conduction system issues."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Criteria Histological confirmation Biomarker, ECG, or imaging"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"abnormalities consistent with myocarditis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Possible subclinical acute Absent Needed myocarditis In the clinical context of possible myocardial injury without cardiovascular symptoms but with at least one of the following: Biomarkers of cardiac injury raised ECG findings suggestive of cardiac injury Abnormal cardiac function on echocardiogram or cardiac MRI"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Probable acute myocarditis Absent Needed In the clinical context of possible myocardial injury with cardiovascular symptoms and at least one of the following: Biomarkers of cardiac injury raised ECG findings suggestive of cardiac injury Abnormal cardiac function on echocardiogram or cardiac MRI"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Definite myocarditis Histological or Not Needed"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"immunohistological evidence of myocarditis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Identify the disease?","o":[{"t":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral stenosis"},{"t":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Mitral stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given specimen is of mitral stenosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The mitral valve opening is reduced in area and has a \"fish mouth\" appearance . It is difficult to assess the left ventricle from this specimen, but there may be a degree of left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The mitral valve opening is reduced in area and has a \"fish mouth\" appearance . It is difficult to assess the left ventricle from this specimen, but there may be a degree of left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4372]},{"n":3,"q":"A 65 year old male patient presented with chest pain, exertional dyspnea and syncope. He was diagnosed as a case of aortic stenosis and his chest x-ray was taken which is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Dilated aortic root"},{"t":"Widening of aortic knob"},{"t":"Widening of vascular pedicle"},{"t":"Post stenotic dilatation of aorta"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Post stenotic dilatation of aorta:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical presentation of chest pain, exertional dyspnea, and syncope, along with the diagnosis of aortic stenosis and chest x-ray findings, the most likely diagnosis in this case is Post stenotic dilatation of the aorta. The correct answer is: Post stenotic dilatation of aorta"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aortic stenosis is a condition characterized by the narrowing of the aortic valve, leading to obstruction of blood flow from the left ventricle into the aorta. This can cause symptoms such as chest pain, exertional dyspnea, and syncope due to the decreased cardiac output and increased pressure load on the left ventricle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post stenotic dilatation of the aorta refers to the enlargement or dilation of the aorta just distal to the stenotic aortic valve. It occurs as a compensatory mechanism in response to the increased pressure gradient across the narrowed valve. The dilatation allows for better forward blood flow and helps to reduce the afterload on the left ventricle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"On a chest x-ray, post stenotic dilatation of the aorta can be visualized as an enlargement of the aortic contour immediately distal to the stenotic valve. It typically appears as a bulbous or widened appearance of the aorta."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[4378]},{"n":4,"q":"A 5 year old child has flexible fingers and can even touch the back of his hand with his wrist extended. The child also demonstrates the sign shown in the image below. On auscultation, a click is heard after S1, followed by a late systolic murmur. What is the probable diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome"},{"t":"Marfan’s syndrome"},{"t":"Loeys-Dietz syndrome"},{"t":"Osteogenesis imperfecta"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Ehlers-Danlos syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (EDS) is a group of genetic disorders that affect the connective tissues in the body. It is characterized by various systemic manifestations, primarily involving the skin, joints, and blood vessels. Based on the given clinical features of joint hypermobility, the ability to touch the back of the hand with the wrist extended, tongue to nose sign, and auscultatory findings of mitral valve prolapse the likely diagnosis is EDS."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4381]},{"n":5,"q":"The device seen in the following image is?","o":[{"t":"Cardiac pacemaker"},{"t":"Chemo port device"},{"t":"Travel tract for accessory"},{"t":"Chest x ray artefact"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Cardiac Pacemaker:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A cardiac pacemaker is a device implanted in the chest to regulate the heart's electrical activity. On a chest X-ray, a cardiac pacemaker appears as a small, metallic object usually located in the upper chest area or near the heart. It may appear as a well-defined structure with lead wires extending into the heart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4390]},{"n":6,"q":"Please select the incorrect statement regarding the bundle of Kent.","o":[{"t":"It is faster than AV nodal pathway"},{"t":"It is slower than AV nodal pathway"},{"t":"Leads to short PR interval"},{"t":"Leads to prolonged QRS duration"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - It is slower than AV nodal pathway:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is incorrect. The bundle of Kent is actually faster than the AV nodal pathway."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - It is faster than the AV nodal pathway : This statement is correct. The bundle of Kent, being an accessory pathway, can conduct electrical impulses faster than the normal AV nodal pathway."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Leads to short PR interval: The presence of an accessory pathway, such as the bundle of Kent, can lead to a short PR interval on an electrocardiogram (ECG)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Leads to prolonged QRS duration: The bundle of Kent can cause a delta wave on the ECG, which is an early ventricular activation, resulting in a widened or prolonged QRS complex."}]},{"n":7,"q":"What is the diagnosis of this patient from the ECG shown below?","o":[{"t":"Normal ECG"},{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"},{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Misplaced leads"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Ventricular tachycardia :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ventricular tachycardia is characterized by a rapid heart rate originating from the ventricles , resulting in a wide QRS complex. The absence of P waves suggests that the ventricles are not being activated through the normal atrioventricular conduction pathway."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Normal ECG : This option is incorrect because the ECG presented does not represent a normal rhythm. It shows a rapid heart rate, wide QRS complex, and absence of P waves, which are indicative of an abnormal cardiac rhythm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ventricular fibrillation : ventricular fibrillation typically appears as a chaotic, irregular rhythm with no discernible QRS complexes or P waves. The ECG shown in the question displays a regular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, which is more consistent with ventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Misplaced leads : This option is incorrect The ECG pattern shown is consistent with ventricular tachycardia rather than an artifact caused by lead misplacement."}],"qi":[4397]},{"n":8,"q":"Which organism is most likely responsible for the development of prosthetic valve endocarditis in a patient following valve replacement surgery?","o":[{"t":"Staphylococcus epidermidis"},{"t":"Staphylococcus aureus"},{"t":"Coagulase negative staphylococci"},{"t":"HACEK organisms"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Staphylococcus epidermidis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Staphylococcus epidermidis , is most commonly associated with prosthetic valve endocarditis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Staphylococcus aureus: This statement is incorrect. While Staphylococcus aureus is a common cause of infective endocarditis, including native valve endocarditis, it is less commonly associated with prosthetic valve endocarditis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Coagulase-negative staphylococci are more commonly associated with prosthetic valve endocarditis within 12 months."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - HACEK organisms: This statement is incorrect."}]},{"n":9,"q":"Which of the following conditions is linked to AV block?","o":[{"t":"Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Cushing’s syndrome"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"},{"t":"Pheochromocytoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypothyroidism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypothyroidism, a condition characterized by an underactive thyroid gland and reduced production of thyroid hormones, is known to be associated with AV block. The exact mechanism is not fully understood, but it is believed that the low levels of thyroid hormones can have direct effects on the electrical conduction system of the heart, leading to disturbances in the conduction pathway and potentially resulting in AV block."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are not associated with AV block."}]},{"n":10,"q":"Identify the following condition:","o":[{"t":"Hydropneumothorax"},{"t":"Pleural effusion"},{"t":"Pericardial effusion"},{"t":"Pneumothorax"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hydropneumothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hydropneumothorax refers to the presence of both air and fluid in the pleural cavity. On a chest X-ray, it may appear as a radiolucent (dark) area with air-fluid levels seen within the pleural space. This condition can occur in cases of trauma, infection, or underlying lung diseases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4406]},{"n":11,"q":"Resistant hypertension is defined as?","o":[{"t":"BP >160/90 mmHg with one antihypertensive"},{"t":"BP >140/90 mmHg with one antihypertensive"},{"t":"BP >160/90 mmHg with three or more antihypertensive"},{"t":"BP >140/90 mmHg with three or more antihypertensive"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - BP >140/90 mmHg with three or more antihypertensive:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resistant hypertension is defined as persistently elevated blood pressure (BP) that remains above the target level despite the use of three or more antihypertensive medications, including a diuretic, at optimal doses. The BP threshold for defining resistant hypertension is typically set at >140/90 mmHg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The rationale behind this definition is that it distinguishes individuals who are truly resistant to treatment and require further evaluation and management. It indicates a failure to achieve adequate BP control despite multiple therapeutic interventions, which may warrant additional investigations to identify and address underlying causes or contributing factors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":12,"q":"What is the optimal approach for treating a patient who is hemodynamically stable, has a broad QRS complex on their ECG, and is experiencing antidromic tachycardia?","o":[{"t":"Oral verapamil"},{"t":"Oral Beta-blocker"},{"t":"Cardioversion"},{"t":"Intravenous Procainamide"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous procainamide: Procainamide is an antiarrhythmic medication that can be effective in treating antidromic tachycardia with a broad QRS complex. It works by blocking certain cardiac ion channels and restoring normal rhythm. Intravenous administration allows for rapid onset of action and is the preferred treatment option in this scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options: Option A. Oral verapamil: Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that can be effective in treating certain types of supraventricular tachycardias. However, in the case of antidromic tachycardia with a broad QRS complex, verapamil is not recommended as it can potentially worsen the condition. Option B. Oral beta-blocker: Beta-blockers are commonly used for rate control in supraventricular tachycardias. However, in the case of antidromic tachycardia with a broad QRS complex, oral beta-blockers may not be effective and are not the first-line treatment choice. Option C . Cardioversion: Cardioversion involves the electrical conversion ofan abnormal heart rhythm back to normal sinus rhythm. While cardioversion may be appropriatein certain situations, it is not the first-line treatment for antidromic tachycardia with a broad QRScomplex."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Which of the following is an incorrect procedure when dealing with W.P.W?\n(or)\nWhich of the following is not done in W.P.W?","o":[{"t":"Treadmill test"},{"t":"Electrophysiological studies"},{"t":"Oral beta blocker"},{"t":"Procainamide"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A: Treadmill test"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treadmill test, also known as exercise stress testing, is not typically performed in individuals with Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome. WPW is a condition characterized by an abnormal electrical pathway (accessory pathway) in the heart that can cause rapid heart rates and potentially dangerous arrhythmias. Exercise stress testing may not be recommended in WPW because it can potentially induce tachycardias or ventricular fibrillation due to the presence of the accessory pathway."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Electrophysiological studies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrophysiological studies (EPS) are commonly performed in individuals with WPW syndrome. EPS involves the placement of catheters inside the heart to study the electrical conduction system and identify the location of the accessory pathway. It helps in determining the risk of arrhythmias and guiding further treatment options."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Oral beta blocker"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta blockers are often used in the management of WPW syndrome. They work by blocking the effects of adrenaline on the heart, helping to slow down the heart rate and reduce the risk of arrhythmias. Oral beta blockers are frequently prescribed to control heart rate and prevent arrhythmias in individuals with WPW."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . Procainamide"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Procainamide is an antiarrhythmic medication that is sometimes used in the acute treatment of arrhythmias associated with WPW syndrome. It works by slowing down the electrical conduction through the accessory pathway. However, its use is generally reserved for acute situations and may not be the first-line treatment option."}]},{"n":14,"q":"For which of the following conditions is rescue percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) performed?","o":[{"t":"Persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 60 min after thrombolysis"},{"t":"Persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 30 min after thrombolysis"},{"t":"Persistent chest persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 90 min after thrombolysis"},{"t":"Pain with ST elevation for > 120 min after thrombolysis"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option C"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 90 min after thrombolysis: This option is correct. Rescue PCI is indicated when there is persistent chest pain accompanied by ST elevation more than 90 minutes after thrombolysis. In this case, the patient has ongoing ischemia despite thrombolysis, and urgent PCI is necessary to restore blood flow to the affected coronary artery. Therefore, option C is correct."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . Persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 60 min after thrombolysis: This option is incorrect. Rescue PCI is not typically performed for chest pain persisting for 60 minutes after thrombolysis. In general, the window of opportunity for rescue PCI is shorter, and intervention is more effective when performed earlier."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Persistent chest pain with ST elevation > 30 min after thrombolysis: This option is incorrect. Similarly to option A, rescue PCI is not typically performed for chest pain persisting for 30 minutes after thrombolysis. Earlier intervention is generally preferred."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . Pain with ST elevation for > 120 min after thrombolysis: This option is incorrect. Although the duration of chest pain and ST elevation is longer in this option, rescue PCI is typically performed when there is persistent chest pain and ST elevation more than 90 minutes after thrombolysis. Waiting for 120 minutes may delay necessary intervention"}]},{"n":15,"q":"What are the effects on the fetus when Indomethacin is used in the uterus during the third trimester?","o":[{"t":"Patent ductus arteriosus"},{"t":"Early closure of ductus arteriosus"},{"t":"Ventricular septal defect"},{"t":"Atrial septal defect"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option B"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Early closure of ductus arteriosus: Indomethacin is a prostaglandin inhibitor, and its use in utero can lead to premature closure of the ductus arteriosus before birth, which is the intended effect in certain cases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . Patent ductus arteriosus: Indomethacin is actually used to treat patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in premature infants. PDA is a condition where the ductus arteriosus fails to close after birth. So, the use of Indomethacin would not lead to PDA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C . Ventricular septal defect: Ventricular septal defect (VSD) is a congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening between the ventricles. The use of Indomethacin is not associated with the development of VSD."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . Atrial septal defect: Atrial septal defect (ASD) is a congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening between the atria. The use of Indomethacin is not associated with the development of ASD."}]},{"n":16,"q":"Continuous murmur is heard in?","o":[{"t":"Patent ductus arteriosus"},{"t":"VSD"},{"t":"ASD"},{"t":"Tetralogy of Fallout"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A: Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Continuous murmurs are not confined to either phase of the cardiac cycle but instead begin in early systole and proceed through S2 into all or part of diastole."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small PDAs are often silent to auscultation, and do not cause hemodynamic changes. The classic murmur is heard best just below the left clavicle and typically extends from systole past the second heart sound into diastole, reflecting flow turbulence and gradient between the aorta and the pulmonary arteries (resulting in left-to-right shunting)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continuous murmurs are not confined to either phase of the cardiac cycle but instead begin in early systole and proceed through S2 into all or part of diastole."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Small PDAs are often silent to auscultation, and do not cause hemodynamic changes. The classic murmur is heard best just below the left clavicle and typically extends from systole past the second heart sound into diastole, reflecting flow turbulence and gradient between the aorta and the pulmonary arteries (resulting in left-to-right shunting)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Ventricular septal defect (VSD): VSD is a common congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening between the right and left ventricles. The murmur associated with VSD is typically a holosystolic (pansystolic) murmur, heard best at the lower left sternal border. It is not a continuous murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C . Atrial septal defect (ASD): ASD is a congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening between the atria. The murmur associated with ASD is typically a systolic ejection murmur, heard best at the upper left sternal border. It is not a continuous murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D . Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF): TOF is a complex congenital heart defect characterized by a combination of four specific heart abnormalities. The murmur associated with TOF is typically a systolic ejection murmur, often accompanied by a harsh or crescendo-decrescendo quality. It is not a continuous murmur."}]},{"n":17,"q":"What is the waist to hip ratio that is associated with an increased risk of heart disease?","o":[{"t":"> 0.80 in males"},{"t":"> 0.80 in females"},{"t":"> 0.85 in males"},{"t":"> 0.85 in females"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"> 0.85 in females: This option is correct. A waist-to-hip ratio greater than 0.85 in females is associated with an increased risk of heart disease. It indicates a higher proportion of abdominal fat and is considered a marker of central obesity, which is linked to cardiovascular health risks."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . > 0.80 in males: While a waist-to-hip ratio greater than 0.80 in males may suggest central obesity, it is not specifically associated with an increased risk of heart disease. The threshold for increased risk is higher in males."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . > 0.80 in females: This option is incorrect. A waist-to-hip ratio greater than 0.80 in females is not the commonly accepted threshold for increased risk of heart disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C . > 0.85 in males: This option is incorrect. While a waist-to-hip ratio greater than 0.80 in males may indicate central obesity, it is not specifically associated with an increased risk of heart disease. The threshold for increased risk is typically lower in males compared to females."}]},{"n":18,"q":"Which of the following is not correct about Defibrillation?","o":[{"t":"Only be used by a trained person"},{"t":"Decrease in success rate with delay in initiation"},{"t":"Improve prognosis"},{"t":"1 minute gap between 2 shocks"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is incorrect. There is typically no fixed time interval between two defibrillation shocks. The decision to deliver subsequent shocks is based on the specific circumstances and the response of the patient. In some cases, it may be necessary to deliver multiple shocks in rapid succession, while in other cases, a pause may be required to assess the patient's response or to provide additional interventions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A . Only be used by a trained person: This option is correct. Defibrillation is a medical procedure that involves delivering an electric shock to the heart to restore its normal rhythm. It is a complex procedure that typically requires training and certification. Using a defibrillator without proper training can be dangerous and potentially harmful."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B . Decrease in success rate with delay in initiation: This option is correct. Defibrillation is most effective when it is performed promptly after identifying a cardiac arrest. The longer the delay in initiating defibrillation, the lower the success rate becomes. Early defibrillation is crucial for improving the chances of survival in cardiac arrest cases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C . Improve prognosis: This option is correct. Defibrillation is a critical intervention in cases of cardiac arrest caused by ventricular fibrillation (VF) or ventricular tachycardia (VT). Delivering an electric shock through defibrillation can restore a normal heart rhythm, which significantly improves the prognosis or outcome for the individual experiencing the cardiac arrest."}]},{"n":19,"q":"Which of the following treatment options is not suitable for managing a patient with non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)?","o":[{"t":"Aspirin"},{"t":"Clopidogrel"},{"t":"Streptokinase"},{"t":"Prasugrel"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient with non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), streptokinase is not typically used for treatment. Streptokinase belongs to the class of fibrinolytic agents and is commonly used for the management of ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) to dissolve blood clots causing the blockage in the coronary arteries. However, in NSTEMI, the underlying mechanism is often different, and the management approach focuses on antiplatelet therapy and anticoagulation rather than fibrinolysis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Aspirin: Aspirin is a fundamental component of the treatment for NSTEMI. It is an antiplatelet agent that inhibits platelet aggregation and helps prevent further clot formation. Aspirin is commonly prescribed in the acute phase of NSTEMI and continued as long-term therapy to reduce the risk of recurrent cardiovascular events."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Clopidogrel: Clopidogrel is another antiplatelet medication that is commonly used in the management of NSTEMI. It belongs to the class of P2Y12 inhibitors and helps inhibit platelet activation and aggregation. Clopidogrel is often prescribed in combination with aspirin for dual antiplatelet therapy to further reduce the risk of thrombotic events."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Prasugrel: Prasugrel is a potent antiplatelet medication that is also used in the management of NSTEMI. It is a newer P2Y12 inhibitor that provides more potent platelet inhibition compared to clopidogrel. Prasugrel is typically reserved for patients who undergo percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) or have a high risk of recurrent ischemic events."}]},{"n":20,"q":"What should be the next course of action in managing a patient who presents to the emergency room with symptoms of palpitation? The patient's pulse is measured at 180 beats per minute and their blood pressure is 70 mmHg systolic. The electrocardiogram (ECG) reveals a narrow QRS complex tachycardia with a regular heart rate.","o":[{"t":"DC cardioversion"},{"t":"Adenosine"},{"t":"Valsalva Maneuver"},{"t":"Verapamil"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient presenting with narrow QRS complex tachycardia, a regular heart rate, and signs of hemodynamic instability (low blood pressure), immediate cardioversion is the most appropriate next step in management."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DC cardioversion involves delivering a synchronized electric shock to the heart in order to restore normal sinus rhythm. It is the treatment of choice for unstable tachyarrhythmias, such as in this scenario where the patient has a significantly elevated heart rate and low blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Adenosine: Adenosine is commonly used for the acute termination of supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) episodes. However, it is not the preferred initial treatment for a patient with signs of hemodynamic instability and low blood pressure. Adenosine can cause a transient period of asystole when administered, which can further compromise the patient's blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Valsalva Maneuver: The Valsalva Maneuver, which involves straining against a closed glottis, can be attempted as a non-invasive method to terminate certain types of supraventricular tachycardias. However, in this scenario where the patient is hemodynamically unstable with low blood pressure, immediate intervention with DC cardioversion is required."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Verapamil: Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that can be effective in terminating certain types of supraventricular tachycardias, such as atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. However, in a hemodynamically unstable patient with low blood pressure,immediate electrical cardioversion is the preferred and more effective approach."}]},{"n":21,"q":"What would be the most appropriate management approach for a patient who arrives at the hospital within 3 hours of experiencing chest pain, where an ECG shows ST depression in anterior chest leads and T wave inversion?","o":[{"t":"Intra aortic balloon pump(IABP)"},{"t":"Thrombolysis with alteplase"},{"t":"Prophylaxis for arrhythmia"},{"t":"Aspirin with heparin"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient presented within 3 hours of the onset of chest pain, which suggests an acute coronary syndrome (ACS). The ECG findings of ST depression in anterior chest leads and T wave inversion are consistent with myocardial ischemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the patient is a candidate for early antithrombotic therapy to prevent further ischemic events and complications. Aspirin and heparin are commonly used in the initial management of ACS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aspirin is an antiplatelet agent that inhibits platelet aggregation, reducing the risk of clot formation. It is administered promptly to patients suspected of having ACS to minimize ongoing thrombosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heparin is an anticoagulant that helps prevent the formation and propagation of blood clots. It is often used in combination with aspirin in the early management of ACS to provide additional anticoagulant effects."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention): PCI is a preferred treatment option for patients with ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI). However, in this case, the ECG findings indicate ST depression and T wave inversion, which are suggestive of non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI) or unstable angina. PCI is not the first-line treatment for NSTEMI or unstable angina within the initial hours of symptom onset."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Thrombolysis with alteplase: Thrombolysis, or clot-dissolving therapy, is primarily indicated for patients with STEMI who present within a specific time window and are not candidates for primary PCI. In this case, the patient's ECG findings suggest NSTEMI or unstable angina, and thrombolysis is not the preferred treatment for these conditions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Prophylaxis for arrhythmia: While arrhythmias can be a complication of ACS, prophylactic treatment for arrhythmias is not the primary management strategy in the acute phase. The focus initially is on addressing the underlying ischemia and preventing further thrombosis through antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy."}]},{"n":22,"q":"A 45-year-old female patient presents to the clinic with complaints of increasing fatigue and shortness of breath. Upon auscultation, the physician detects a pan systolic murmur that is best heard at the right side of the heart. The murmur radiates to the axilla and is louder during inspiration. The patient denies any history of fever, chest pain, or recent infections. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient's condition based on the described findings?","o":[{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral valve prolapse"},{"t":"Ventricular septal defect"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D) Tricuspid Regurgitation: Tricuspid regurgitation is characterized by a pan systolic murmur heard best along the right side of the heart and it radiates to the right of the sternum. However, the murmur does not typically radiate to the axilla. Additionally, tricuspid regurgitation is often secondary to other conditions affecting the right side of the heart, such as pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure, which might lead to clinical signs not mentioned in the case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Tricuspid Regurgitation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In Summary: The characteristics of a pan systolic murmur heard at the right side of the heart and radiating to the axilla, along with the absence of signs of aortic stenosis, mitral valve prolapse, and ventricular septal defect, point toward tricuspid regurgitation as the most likely diagnosis in this case. It's important to consider the entire clinical picture and potentially perform further tests (e.g., echocardiography) to confirm the diagnosis and assess the severity of the condition."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The key points, in this case, are the characteristics of the murmur (pan systolic, right sided location, radiation to axilla, louder during inspiration) and the absence of fever or infections, which helps to differentiate the options."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A) Aortic Stenosis: Aortic stenosis typically presents with a crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border, and it often radiates to the carotid arteries. This does not match the characteristics of the murmur described in the case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B) Mitral Valve Prolapse: Mitral valve prolapse is characterized by a mid-systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur. The murmur usually occurs after the click and is not pan systolic. Additionally, mitral valve prolapse is not typically associated with radiation of the murmur to the axilla."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C) Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD): A VSD would cause a harsh, holosystolic murmur heard best at the left lower sternal border, as blood flows from the higher-pressure left ventricle to the lower-pressure right ventricle. The characteristics and location of the murmur in this case do not match those of a VSD."}]},{"n":23,"q":"Correct sequence after p wave: A- ‘a’ wave B – 1st heart sound C – Rapid filling of ventricles D – ‘t’ wave","o":[{"t":"ABCD"},{"t":"ABDC"},{"t":"CABD"},{"t":"ACBD"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The correct sequence after the P wave is as follows:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A. 'a' wave: The 'a' wave represents atrial contraction. It occurs during atrial systole when the atria contract to push blood into the ventricles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B. 1st heart sound: The 1st heart sound, also known as S1, is the closure of the atrioventricular (AV) valves (mitral and tricuspid valves) at the beginning of ventricular systole. It marks the onset of ventricular contraction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D. 't' wave: The 't' wave is a repolarization phase of the ventricles. It represents the relaxation and resetting of the ventricles after contraction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C. Rapid filling of ventricles: After the 't' wave, the ventricles enter the diastole phase, during which they passively fill with blood from the atria. This is known as rapid ventricular filling."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the correct sequence is ABDC: 'a' wave, 1st heart sound, rapid filling of ventricles, and 't' wave."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: ACBD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: . CABD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A) and the 1st heart sound (B). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: ACBD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D)."}]},{"n":24,"q":"A 68-year-old male admitted to the ICU for acute exacerbation of COPD develops a sudden onset of palpitations. The following ECG changes are seen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Multifocal atrial tachycardia"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial tachycardia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Multifocal atrial tachycardia (MAT) is characterized by an irregular rhythm with at least three distinct P-wave morphologies in the same lead. The ECG changes described in the scenario, specifically the presence of multiple P-wave morphologies, are consistent with MAT."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Ventricular tachycardia (VT) is characterized by wide QRS complexes and is usually a regular rhythm. The ECG shown in the scenario does not exhibit the characteristics of VT."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Atrial fibrillation (AF) is characterized by irregularly irregular rhythm with absent P waves and irregularly spaced QRS complexes. The scenario does not describe the absence of P waves, making AF less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Atrial tachycardia is characterized by a regular rhythm with a single P-wave morphology. The presence of multiple P-wave morphologies in the ECG suggests a different diagnosis than atrial tachycardia."}],"qi":[4415]},{"n":25,"q":"Deep y descent in JVP is seen in all except:","o":[{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Cardiac tamponade typically presents with an elevated JVP but a blunted or absent y descent due to impaired filling of the right ventricle during diastole. Therefore, deep y descent is not seen in cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterized by stiffening of the heart muscle, leading to impaired ventricular filling. It can result in a prominent and rapid y descent in the JVP waveform."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Constrictive pericarditis involves the thickening and stiffening of the pericardium, leading to impaired cardiac filling. It is associated with a prominent and rapid y descent in the JVP waveform."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Tricuspid regurgitation is a condition in which the tricuspid valve fails to close properly, causing blood to flow backward into the right atrium during systole. This leads to an elevated JVP and a large v wave. Steep y descent is seen"}]},{"n":26,"q":"True about the definition of postural hypotension:","o":[{"t":"Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 6 mins of standing"},{"t":"Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 min of standing"},{"t":"Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 6 mins of standing"},{"t":"Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 mins of standing"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 min of standing: This option is correct. Postural hypotension, also known as orthostatic hypotension, is defined as a decrease in systolic blood pressure of 20 mmHg or more or a decrease in diastolic blood pressure of 10 mmHg or more within 3 minutes of standing up from a sitting or lying position"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 6 mins of standing: This option is incorrect. The definition of postural hypotension does not specify a specific duration of standing as 6 minutes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C : Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 6 mins of standing: This option is incorrect. The definition of postural hypotension does not specify a specific duration of standing as 6 minutes, and it focuses on the systolic blood pressure drop rather than the diastolic blood pressure drop."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mmHg after 3 mins of standing: This option is incorrect. While a decrease in diastolic blood pressure can contribute to postural hypotension, the primary criterion for its diagnosis is a decrease in systolic blood pressure of 20 mmHg or more."}]},{"n":27,"q":"The ECG of a pregnant lady having pre-eclampsia is shown below. Her vitals are stable. What is the best step in the management of the condition?","o":[{"t":"IV calcium"},{"t":"IV MgSO4"},{"t":"DC shock"},{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option b. IV MgSO4 (Magnesium sulfate): Magnesium sulfate is the preferred treatment for torsades des pointes, especially in the setting of pre-eclampsia. Magnesium sulfate has antiarrhythmic properties and can effectively stabilize the cardiac rhythm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option a. IV calcium: Intravenous calcium is not the recommended treatment for torsades des pointes. It is more commonly used in the management of calcium channel blocker overdose or hypocalcemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option c. DC shock: Direct current (DC) shock, also known as defibrillation, is not the primary treatment for torsades des pointes. It is typically reserved for life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias such as ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Option d. Synchronized cardioversion: Synchronized cardioversion is not the appropriate treatment for torsades des pointes. It is used for the conversion of certain supraventricular tachycardias and hemodynamically unstable ventricular arrhythmias."}],"qi":[4421]},{"n":28,"q":"What should be the subsequent course of action in managing a patient with the following ECG, who has already undergone carotid massage and received IV adenosine, but currently has a blood pressure reading of 60/30 mm Hg upon presentation to the emergency room?","o":[{"t":"Repeat inj adenosine 6 mg"},{"t":"Inj amiodarone 300 mg"},{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"},{"t":"DC cardioversion"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Synchronized cardioversion: Synchronized cardioversion is the next best step of management in this scenario. It involves delivering a synchronized electrical shock to the heart at a specific moment in the cardiac cycle to restore normal sinus rhythm. It is an effective method for terminating PSVT and can rapidly improve the patient's condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A. Repeat inj adenosine 6 mg: Adenosine is a medication used to treat supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). However, since the patient has already received adenosine and it did not result in the termination of the arrhythmia, repeating the same dose is unlikely to be effective."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Inj amiodarone 300 mg: Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic medication that can be used for various types of arrhythmias. However, in this case, the patient's blood pressure is already low, and amiodarone may further lower the blood pressure, potentially causing hemodynamic instability."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. DC cardioversion: DC cardioversion, also known as defibrillation, is not appropriate for this patient as it is used to treat life-threatening arrhythmias such as ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia. In PSVT, which is a regular but rapid rhythm, synchronized cardioversion is the preferred approach."}],"qi":[4424]},{"n":29,"q":"What is the most likely diagnosis for an adult patient with a pulsatile swelling in the neck below the jaw angle that causes an outward expansile impulse, based on the findings of an MRI angiography?","o":[{"t":"Grave’s disease"},{"t":"Branchial cyst"},{"t":"Thymic cyst"},{"t":"Carotid body tumour"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D: Carotid body tumour"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above case, there is a pulsatile swelling, and on MRI angiography, it shows Lyre,s sign which is splaying of internal and external arteries , which is diagnostic of carotid body tumour. It is a slow-growing tumour and presents mainly after 40 years of age."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Grave’s disease is a hyperthyroid condition with bulging eyes, and the neck swelling is not pulsatile."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Branchial cysts are small fluid-filled sacs that look like lumps under the skin on the side of the neck and are more common in children than adults."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Thymic cyst is a benign mediastinal disease and presents with dyspnea, chest pain, cough, hoarseness and dysphagia."}],"qi":[4430]},{"n":30,"q":"Identify the tachycardia depicted in the given ECG.","o":[{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial flutter"},{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B: Atrial flutter"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above ECG is that of atrial flutter as it shows narrow QRS complexes and sawtooth-shaped waves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: In atrial fibrillation, the QRS complexes are irregularly irregular, is RR intervals vary."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Ventricular tachycardia has broad QRS complexes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Ventricular Fibrillation has no identifiable P wave or QRS complexes."}],"qi":[4436]},{"n":31,"q":"Identify the diagnosis:","o":[{"t":"Normal ECG"},{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"},{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Misplaced leads"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: Ventricular tachycardia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above ECG is ventricular tachycardia as it has a wide QRS complex. P waves are absent, and the heart rate is over 100 beats per minute."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: The above is not a normal ECG as it has tachycardia, absent P wave, and vast QRS complexes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Ventricular fibrillation has no identifiable QRS complex."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: This is not the case for misplaced leads."}],"qi":[4442]},{"n":32,"q":"What is the most likely diagnosis for a 45-year-old man who experiences chest pain and difficulty breathing that worsens when lying down but improves when sitting upright, and has muffled heart sounds during examination?","o":[{"t":"Pericardial effusion"},{"t":"Tetralogy of Fallot"},{"t":"Transposition of great arteries"},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A: Pericardial effusion"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above presentation of breathlessness on lying down and it being relieved on sitting, and the x-ray given which shows an enlarged cardiac silhouette is suggestive of a probable diagnosis of pericardial effusion. It is also known as a money bag or leather bottle-shaped heart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Tetralogy of Fallot shows a boot-shaped heart on an x-ray."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Transposition of great arteries shows an egg-on-string appearance on x-ray."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Pulmonary hypertension shows a pruned tree appearance of the pulmonary vasculature."}],"qi":[4448]},{"n":33,"q":"Which is the best confirmatory method to ensure the central line is in the jugular vein?","o":[{"t":"Chest x-ray"},{"t":"Blood pH"},{"t":"Blood color"},{"t":"ETCO2"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A: Chest x-ray"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray is the best confirmatory method to ensure the central line is in the jugular vein."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Blood pH is inconclusive and not the best confirmatory method."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Blood color is very subjective and may be misleading."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: ETCO2 is not used in this case."}]},{"n":34,"q":"What is the purpose of using the Modified Duke Criteria in diagnosing?","o":[{"t":"Infective endarteritis"},{"t":"Infectious mononucleosis"},{"t":"Inflammatory myopathy"},{"t":"Infective endocarditis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Modified Duke criteria are widely used criteria for the diagnosis of infective endocarditis, which is an infection of the endocardium (inner lining) of the heart, including heart valves. The criteria help in establishing a diagnosis based on clinical, laboratory, and echocardiographic findings. They take into account major and minor criteria, as well as the presence of predisposing factors, to classify the likelihood of infective endocarditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Major Criteria Description Positive Blood Culture - Typical organisms isolated from 2 separate blood cultures. - Persistently positive blood cultures, with typical organisms isolated from cultures done > 12 hours apart. - Coxiella infection (Q fever) demonstrated by a single positive blood culture with Phase I IgG titre > 1:800. Echocardiographic Evidence - Evidence of vegetation or oscillating intracardiac mass on echocardiography. - Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is preferred over transthoracic echocardiography (TTE). - New valvular regurgitation or partial dehiscence of prosthetic valve. New Onset Murmur in Patient with Pre-existing Heart Lesion - Development of a new heart murmur in a patient with pre-existing heart disease. Minor Modified Criteria Description Predisposition - Presence of pre-existing heart disease or history of intravenous drug usage. Fever > 38°C - Fever with temperature exceeding 38°C. Vascular Phenomenon - Major embolic phenomenon. - Septic pulmonary infarction. - Mycotic aneurysm. - Intracranial hemorrhage. - Janeway lesions (palm, soles). Immunological Phenomenon (Mnemonic – ROG) - Roth spots. - Rheumatoid factor positivity. - Osler nodes (tip of fingers). - Glomerulonephritis. Microbiological Evidence - Positive blood culture not meeting major criteria. - Moderate-high clinical suspicion warrants transesophageal echocardiography (TEE), while low suspicion warrants transthoracic echocardiography (TTE). - Additional diagnostic modalities include SHOT metagenomics,FDG PET-CT, and multi-slice CT angiography."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Major Criteria Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Positive Blood Culture - Typical organisms isolated from 2 separate blood"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cultures. - Persistently positive blood cultures, with typical organisms isolated from cultures done > 12 hours apart. - Coxiella infection (Q fever) demonstrated by a single positive blood culture with Phase I IgG titre > 1:800."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Echocardiographic Evidence - Evidence of vegetation or oscillating intracardiac mass"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"on echocardiography. - Transesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is preferred over transthoracic echocardiography (TTE). - New valvular regurgitation or partial dehiscence of prosthetic valve."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"New Onset Murmur in Patient with - Development of a new heart murmur in a patient with Pre-existing Heart Lesion pre-existing heart disease."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Minor Modified Criteria Description"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Predisposition - Presence of pre-existing heart disease or history of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"intravenous drug usage."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Fever > 38°C - Fever with temperature exceeding 38°C."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Vascular Phenomenon - Major embolic phenomenon. - Septic pulmonary"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"infarction. - Mycotic aneurysm. - Intracranial hemorrhage. - Janeway lesions (palm, soles)."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Immunological Phenomenon - Roth spots. - Rheumatoid factor positivity. - Osler (Mnemonic – ROG) nodes (tip of fingers). - Glomerulonephritis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Microbiological Evidence - Positive blood culture not meeting major criteria. -"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Moderate-high clinical suspicion warrants transesophageal echocardiography (TEE), while low suspicion warrants transthoracic echocardiography (TTE). - Additional diagnostic modalities include SHOT metagenomics, FDG PET-CT, and multi-slice CT angiography."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A (Infective endarteritis) is incorrect because infective endarteritis is not a recognized term or condition. It may be a confusion with infective endocarditis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B (Infectious mononucleosis) is incorrect because the Modified Duke criteria are not used for the diagnosis of infectious mononucleosis, which is caused by the Epstein-Barr virus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C (Inflammatory myopathy) is incorrect because the Modified Duke criteria are not used for the diagnosis of inflammatory myopathy, which refers to a group of muscle disorders characterized by muscle inflammation."}]},{"n":35,"q":"Which medication could be the cause of recurrent falls in an elderly male patient?","o":[{"t":"Prazocin"},{"t":"Metformin"},{"t":"Acarbose"},{"t":"Thiazide"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prazosin is an alpha-1 blocker medication used primarily for the treatment of hypertension and benign prostatic hyperplasia. However, one of the common side effects of prazosin is orthostatic hypotension, which can increase the risk of falls, especially in the elderly. Orthostatic hypotension refers to a sudden drop in blood pressure upon standing, leading to dizziness or lightheadedness, and can result in falls."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Metformin is a medication used for the management of diabetes mellitus, and it is not typically associated with an increased risk of falls."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Acarbose is an alpha-glucosidase inhibitor used in the treatment of diabetes mellitus. It does not commonly cause orthostatic hypotension or an increased risk of falls."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Thiazide refers to a class of diuretic medications commonly used for blood pressure control. While they can sometimes cause orthostatic hypotension, it is not a common side effect compared to prazosin."}]},{"n":36,"q":"A person with dyspnea has a slight limitation in physical activity and doing ordinary activity causes symptoms but he is asymptomatic at rest. To which class does he belong, based on the New York heart association functional classification?","o":[{"t":"Class I"},{"t":"Class II"},{"t":"Class III"},{"t":"Class IV"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B: Class II"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As the patient has dyspnea on light physical activity, there is slight limitation of physical activity. He is asymptomatic at rest. This is Class II in NYHA classification."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Class I has no limitations and no symptoms with ordinary exertion or day to day daily physical activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Class III has marked limitation of physical activity and less than ordinary activities cause symptoms. The individual is asymptomatic at rest."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Class IV has inability to carry out any physical activity without discomfort and symptoms are present even at rest."}]},{"n":37,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a 55-year-old male patient who came to the hospital complaining of palpitations, and during examination, a diastolic murmur is detected in the left third intercostal space, along with the presence of a pistol shot sound over the femoral arteries?","o":[{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A: Aortic regurgitation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above patient presents with the diastolic murmur and the pistol shot over femoral arteries which is suggestive of aortic regurgitation. There is a backflow of blood from the aorta to the ventricle at the time of diastole."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Aortic stenosis presents with a midsystolic ejection murmur at the upper sternal border."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Tricuspid regurgitation presents with a holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Mitral regurgitation presents with a systolic murmur at the apex of the heart with radiation to the left axilla."}]},{"n":38,"q":"Which of the following statements about the culture taken in a suspected case of infective endocarditis is correct?","o":[{"t":"Two samples from peripheral lines 30 min apart"},{"t":"Three samples from peripheral lines 1 hour apart"},{"t":"One sample only from peripheral line"},{"t":"Two peripheral samples, two days apart"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Three samples from peripheral lines 1 hour apart:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blood Cultures in a patient suspected of infective endocarditis who has not received antibiotics during the prior 2 weeks, three two-bottle blood culture sets containing the appropriate volume of blood (10 mL per bottle) should be obtained from different fi venipuncture sites over 1-2 hours. If the cultures remain negative after 48-72 h, two or three additional blood culture sets should be obtained, and the laboratory should be consulted foradvice regarding optimal culture techniques."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Two samples from peripheral lines 30 min apart: This option is incorrect as 3 samples need to be taken 1 hour apart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - One sample only from peripheral line: This option is incorrect as one sample is not sufficient and 3 samples need to be taken 1 hour apart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Two samples, two days apart - This option is incorrect as there is no specification about the timing of sampling."}]},{"n":39,"q":"10-year-old boy's father noticed agonal respiration at 5 AM in sleep . He was rushed to the ER where ECG was performed, What could be the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Early repolarization syndrome"},{"t":"Brugada Syndrome"},{"t":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Brugada Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coved ST-segment elevation >2mm in >1 of V1-V3 followed by a negative T wave is potentially diagnostic of Brugada syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brugada syndrome is more prevalent in male patients of Southeast Asian descent."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient will require an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Early repolarization syndrome : The ECG shown in this vignette is not suggestive of this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy : Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is usually related to physical activity(sports-related)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Atrial fibrillation : Characteristic features of absent p wave and irregular RR intervals are not seen in this patient's ECG."}],"qi":[4452]},{"n":40,"q":"Comment on the diagnosis from the ECG shown below?","o":[{"t":"First degree heart block"},{"t":"Second-degree infra-nodal heart block"},{"t":"Complete heart block"},{"t":"AV dissociation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Second-degree infra-nodal heart block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In second-degree infranodal heart block, there is a constant PR interval before and after the non-conducted beat. Each P wave is associated with a QRS complex until there is a skipped beat, in which case the p wave is not followed by a QRS complex."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - First-degree heart block: There is no change in PR interval."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Seen in 1. Athletes (High vagal tone )"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Rheumatic fever: Aschoff"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Hemochromatosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Sarcoidosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"5. Endomyocardial fibroelastosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Complete heart block: It is due to progressive failure of the AV node or complete failure of the His - Purkinje system. ECG characteristics are shown below:"},{"k":"img","v":4704},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - AV dissociation: It is a pathological phenomenon in which the atria and ventricles beat independently of each other."}],"qi":[4455]},{"n":41,"q":"75-year-old woman with Type 2 DM and hypertension and developed features of heart failure and expired. Post-mortem cardiac biopsy slide is given below?","o":[{"t":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct Options B - Restrictive cardiomyopathy:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The given image is suggestive of restrictive cardiomyopathy, as there is pink amyloid material present in between the myocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Restrictive Cardiomyopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rarest form of cardiomyopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"STIFF HEART: Normal size ventricles but atria is dilated due to having to contract against stiff ventricles"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pink Hyaline deposits (amyloid protein) in the myocardium cause the stiffness of the ventricles."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A - Dilated Cardiomyopathy: This option is incorrect. The histopathological specimen in patients with this condition shows multinucleated myocytes."},{"k":"img","v":4707},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy: Myocyte disarray on histopathological examination is a classic feature of Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."},{"k":"img","v":4710},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Takotsubo cardiomyopathy: Histopathological findings in patients with this type of cardiomyopathy is characterized by interstitial infiltrates of lymphocytes, leukocytes and macrophages. Myocardial fibrosis and contraction bands with or without myocyte necrosis is also seen in these specimens."},{"k":"img","v":4712}],"qi":[4459]},{"n":42,"q":"A diabetic male with chest pain and palpitations came to the ER. ECG was taken, which showed NSTEMI. Treatment?","o":[{"t":"Aspirin + Clopidogrel"},{"t":"Aspirin + Statin"},{"t":"Aspirin + Clopidogrel + LMWH"},{"t":"Aspirin + LMWH"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Aspirin + Clopidogrel + LMWH:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Since the pathology in NSTEMI is a platelet-raised thrombus, the patient is treated with dual antiplatelet therapy along with low molecular weight heparin. LMWH is preferred over unfractionated heparin since it does not require monitoring of partial thromboplastin time and is easier to administer. Thrombolysis is not indicated in NSTEMI."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Aspirin + Clopidogrel: This option is incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Aspirin + Statin: This option is incorrect since dual antiplatelet therapy along with LMWH is the initial treatment of choice."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Aspirin + LMWH: This option is incorrect."}]},{"n":43,"q":"Which of the following is not a component of metabolic syndrome?","o":[{"t":"LDL"},{"t":"Fasting glucose"},{"t":"Abdominal circumference"},{"t":"Blood pressure"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Low LDL:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic features of metabolic syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Abdominal circumference: Globally males > 102cm Globally females > 80cm Indian male > 90 cm Indian females > 80cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Globally males > 102cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Globally females > 80cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indian male > 90 cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indian females > 80cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired glucose tolerance test / insulin resistance: FBS = 100-125 mg/dl 2 hour postprandial = 140-199 mg/dl The patient already on oral hypoglycaemic drugs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FBS = 100-125 mg/dl"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 hour postprandial = 140-199 mg/dl"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient already on oral hypoglycaemic drugs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increase in peripheral resistance, hypertension > 130/85 mmHg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased triglycerides > 150 mg%"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HDL decreased (below 40-50 mg%)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Globally males > 102cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Globally females > 80cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indian male > 90 cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Indian females > 80cm"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FBS = 100-125 mg/dl"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 hour postprandial = 140-199 mg/dl"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient already on oral hypoglycaemic drugs"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}]},{"n":44,"q":"Which of the following jvp findings seen in a patient with muffled heart sounds, hypotension and raised JVP?","o":[{"t":"Absent x descent"},{"t":"Absent y descent"},{"t":"C waves are enlarged"},{"t":"A waves are absent"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Absent y descent:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Y descent is the drop in JVP during early diastole when the tricuspid valve opens and blood flows from the right atrium to the right ventricle. In cardiac tamponade, the elevated pressure in the right atrium due to compression prevents normal descent of the JVP during diastole, leading to an absent y descent. This is the correct finding for the given scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Absent x descent: X descent is the drop in JVP during ventricular systole. It's seen in conditions where the right atrium is not excessively obstructed or compressed. In cardiac tamponade, the x descent can actually be exaggerated, not absent."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - C waves are enlarged: C waves on the JVP waveform represent the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular contraction. Enlarged C waves are often seen in conditions like tricuspid regurgitation, but they are not specifically associated with the given symptoms of muffled heart sounds, hypotension, and raised JVP."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - A waves are absent: A waves correspond to atrial contraction, which occurs just before the tricuspid valve closes. Absent A waves can be seen in conditions like atrial fibrillation or complete heart block, where the atria are not contracting in a coordinated manner. However, absent A waves would not directly explain the combination of symptoms mentioned in the scenario."}]},{"n":45,"q":"Which of the following is correct regarding Wolf Parkinson's white syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Short PJ Interval"},{"t":"Broad QRS interval"},{"t":"Tall Tented T waves"},{"t":"Prolonged PR interval"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Broad QRS interval:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wolf-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome is a condition in which an abnormal electrical pathway known as an accessory pathway exists between the atria and ventricles of the heart. ECG finding associated with Wolf-Parkinson-White syndrome is a short PR interval and a characteristic broad QRS complex due to the abnormal electrical pathway. The presence of the accessory pathway can lead to a delta wave, which is an initial slurring of the QRS complex, causing it to appear broader than normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Short PJ Interval: In WPW syndrome, the characteristic finding is a shortened PR interval rather than a short PJ interval. The PR interval represents the time it takes for the electrical signal to travel from the atria to the ventricles. In WPW syndrome, due to the presence of the accessory pathway, the signal bypasses part of the normal conduction system, resulting in a shorter PR interval on an electrocardiogram (ECG)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tall Tented T Waves: Tall tented T waves are not a typical feature of WPW syndrome. Tall tented T waves are associated with conditions like hyperkalemia (high levels of potassium in the blood) and can be seen in ECGs in those scenarios."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Prolonged PR interval: As mentioned earlier, the PR interval is actually shortened in WPW syndrome due to the accessory pathway bypassing the normal conduction system. A prolonged PR interval could be indicative of other conditions like first-degree heart block."}]},{"n":46,"q":"The patient presented with pedal oedema, raised JVP till mandible and ascites. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Cardiac failure"},{"t":"Renal failure"},{"t":"Liver failure"},{"t":"Brain injury"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Cardiac failure:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The symptoms mentioned - pedal edema, raised jugular venous pressure (JVP) up to the mandible, and ascites - are indicative of congestive heart failure (cardiac failure). Let's go through each option and explain why:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Congestive heart failure occurs when the heart's pumping ability is compromised, leading to fluid accumulation in various parts of the body. Pedal edema (swelling in the feet and ankles) occurs due to fluid retention in the lower extremities. Raised JVP is a sign of increased pressure in the veins returning blood to the heart, often observed in heart failure due to the inefficient pumping of blood. Ascites, the accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity, can also result from the increased venous pressure caused by heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Renal failure: While renal failure can lead to fluid retention and edema, it typically doesn't cause the specific pattern of JVP elevation and ascites seen in the case. Renal failure might lead to edema and fluid overload, but these symptoms are more directly associated with heart failure in this context."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Liver failure: Liver failure can lead to ascites due to disruptions in fluid balance and decreased production of proteins responsible for maintaining proper fluid distribution. However, the elevated JVP up to the mandible is less likely to be caused by liver failure alone. This sign is more indicative of heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Brain injury: Brain injury, unless involving specific areas of the brain that control fluid balance, is not a likely cause of the symptoms described. Brain injury might lead to other neurological symptoms, but it wouldn't explain the combination of pedal edema, raised JVP, and ascites."}]},{"n":47,"q":"A 20 year old male who is an IV drug user. on examination, a temperature of 38.5 C, and some spots can be noted over the fundus. CBC showed a leukocyte count of 20,000/cu mm. Blood has been sent for culture. Infective endocarditis is suspected. How many minor duke's criteria are satisfied for the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"1"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"4"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 3:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duke's criteria satisfied for the diagnosis of infective endocarditis is 3."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Minor Duke's criteria include: Injection drug use Temperature > 38.0°C Phenomena (such as fundus spots)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Injection drug use"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temperature > 38.0°C"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phenomena (such as fundus spots)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Injection drug use"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Temperature > 38.0°C"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phenomena (such as fundus spots)"}]},{"n":48,"q":"What does the image below represent in terms of murmurs?","o":[{"t":"End diastolic"},{"t":"Pan systolic"},{"t":"Ejection systolic"},{"t":"Mid diastolic"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: Ejection systolic"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An ejection systolic murmur occurs during the ejection phase of the cardiac cycle when blood is being pumped out of the heart. It is characterized by a continuous or crescendo-decrescendo sound that starts after the first heart sound (S1) and ends before the second heart sound (S2)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ejection systolic murmur is typically heard best over specific areas of the heart, such as the aortic or pulmonary valve areas, depending on the underlying cause of the murmur. It is caused by turbulent blood flow through a narrowed or abnormal valve or aorta during systole."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: End diastolic: An end diastolic murmur occurs during late diastole, just before the next systole begins. It is commonly associated with conditions such as mitral stenosis or tricuspid regurgitation. The image does not show features or timing consistent with an end diastolic murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Pan systolic: A pan systolic (or holosystolic) murmur is heard throughout systole, starting with or immediately after S1 and continuing until S2. It is typically associated with conditions such as mitral regurgitation or ventricular septal defects. The image does not show features or timing consistent with a pan systolic murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Mid diastolic: A mid diastolic murmur occurs during mid-diastole, after S2 and before S1. It is commonly associated with conditions such as mitral stenosis or tricuspid stenosis. The image does not show features or timing consistent with a mid-diastolic murmur."}],"qi":[4466]},{"n":49,"q":"\"A 45-year-old female presents to the emergency department with breathlessness. She has a history of a viral infection and chest pain from 7 days ago. A chest X-ray reveals a \"water bottle\" heart. All of the following statements are true except:\"","o":[{"t":"Pulsus paradoxus is observed"},{"t":"Loud S1"},{"t":"Emergency pericardiocentesis may be required"},{"t":"ECG showing RA and RV collapse requires immediate intervention"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Loud S1:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the described scenario, viral pericarditis initially occurred, progressing to pericardial effusion and subsequent diastolic collapse of the ventricles, resulting in cardiac tamponade. Due to the presence of fluid surrounding the heart, muffled heart sounds are expected rather than a loud S1."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pulsus paradoxus is observed: Pulsus paradoxus, characterized by a drop in blood pressure of more than 12 mmHg during inspiration, is a feature of cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Emergency pericardiocentesis may be required: This statement is true as pericardiocentesis is performed as an emergency measure as there is diastolic collapse of the ventricles."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - ECG showing RA and RV collapse requires immediate intervention: This statement is true as there is diastolic collapse of ventricles in cardiac tamponade."}]},{"n":50,"q":"Which of the following conditions is indicated by the symptoms of chest pain and breathlessness in a 55-year-old male, as revealed by the provided ECG?","o":[{"t":"Unstable angina"},{"t":"Non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI)"},{"t":"Acute myocarditis"},{"t":"Posterior wall myocardial infarction (MI)"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Posterior wall myocardial infarction (MI):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ST depression is evident in leads V2, V3, and V4, displaying a predominantly horizontal configuration, with upright T waves. Moreover, the R/S ratio in anterior chest leads is greater than 1. These combined observations suggest the likelihood of a posterior wall myocardial infarction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A & B - Unstable angina & NSTEMI: The ECG changes observed in both unstable angina and NSTEMI display similarities and show T wave inversion accompanying ST depression."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute myocarditis: There are no pathognomic findings in acute myocarditis as it shows non-specific ST segment changes."}],"qi":[4472]},{"n":51,"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding a 35-year-old male presenting to the emergency department with unconsciousness and the given ECG findings?","o":[{"t":"Occurrence in individuals with normal heart without any significant pathology is impossible."},{"t":"Direct current cardioversion is universally performed to restore normal rhythm."},{"t":"Intravenous digoxin is the preferred drug."},{"t":"Discrepancy in HR and PR used for diagnosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Direct current cardioversion is universally performed to restore normal rhythm:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG given shows an irregularly irregular RR interval indicating atrial fibrillation. Direct cardioversion is avoided in these cases due to the associated risk of stroke."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Occurrence in individuals with normal heart without any significant pathology is impossible:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is correct as atrial fibrillation is one of the most common arrhythmias seen in individuals with structural damage to the heart rather than with a normal heart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Intravenous digoxin is the preferred drug:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In case of atrial fibrillation with heart failure, Intravenous digoxin will be administered."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Discrepancy in HR and PR used for diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of pulse deficit, that is discrepancy in HR and PR is the most accurate way of diagnosis of the individual. Hence this statement is also correct."}],"qi":[4478]},{"n":52,"q":"In which of the following scenarios is Rescue Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI) indicated?","o":[{"t":"Failed thrombolysis"},{"t":"Chronic stable angina"},{"t":"Prinzmetal's angina"},{"t":"NSTEMI"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Failed thrombolysis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rescue PCI is primarily indicated when thrombolytic therapy (a treatment to dissolve blood clots) has failed. In cases where thrombolysis does not successfully restore blood flow to the heart, PCI is performed as a secondary intervention to alleviate the blockage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Chronic stable angina: Chronic stable angina is a condition where chest pain occurs predictably with exertion or stress. PCI may be considered for symptom relief, but it is not typically classified as a rescue procedure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Prinzmetal's angina: This condition, also known as variant angina, is caused by transient spasm of the coronary arteries. PCI is not usually indicated for Prinzmetal's angina unless there is significant, persistent obstruction that does not resolve with medication."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - NSTEMI: Non-ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI) may require PCI, but it is not typically categorized under rescue PCI. NSTEMI management often involves initial medical treatment, with PCI considered based on the patient's condition and response to medication."}]},{"n":53,"q":"Based on the given ECG, identify which wall of the myocardium was affected in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Inferior wall"},{"t":"Anterior wall"},{"t":"Posterior wall"},{"t":"Lateral wall"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Anterior wall:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ST elevation in leads V2, V3, and V4 indicates an anterior wall myocardial infarction (MI)."},{"k":"img","v":4726},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Inferior wall: Leads indicating inferior wall involvement are Lead II, III, and aVF."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Posterior wall: Posterior wall involvement is indicated by ST depression in leads V1-V4 and ST elevation in leads V7, V8, and V9."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Lateral wall: Lateral wall involvement is shown by changes in leads V5, V6, I, and aVL."}],"qi":[4484]},{"n":54,"q":"A 21-year-old man presents with episodes of chest pain. Physical examination reveals a bisferiens pulse and a heaving apex. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM)"},{"t":"Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM)"},{"t":"Mitral Stenosis"},{"t":"Aortic Stenosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features: HCM is characterized by asymmetric hypertrophy of the ventricular septum, which can cause a dynamic obstruction of the outflow tract. Pulsus bisferiens, a pulse with a double-peaked pattern, and a heaving apex are characteristic physical exam findings in HCM. The condition often involves mutations in genes like MYH7 on chromosome 14."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM): DCM is characterized by left ventricular dilation and impaired systolic function. Physical exam findings commonly include signs of heart failure, such as elevated jugular venous pressure and a displaced apex beat. Pulsus bisferiens and a heaving apex are not typical features of DCM."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mitral Stenosis: Mitral stenosis leads to obstruction of blood flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle. Physical exam findings often include a diastolic murmur best heard at the apex, with possible signs of left atrial enlargement. Pulsus bisferiens and a heaving apex are not typically associated with mitral stenosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Aortic Stenosis: Aortic stenosis leads to obstruction of blood flow from the left ventricle into the aorta. Physical exam findings commonly include a systolic ejection murmur and a reduced or delayed carotid pulse. Pulsus bisferiens is not a typical finding in aortic stenosis; it is more associated with HCM."}]},{"n":55,"q":"A patient of CKD has tachycardia. An ECG is taken and shown below. What’s the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Hypokalemia"},{"t":"Hypercalcemia"},{"t":"Hypocalcemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hyperkalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C hronic kidney disease (CKD) often leads to electrolyte imbalances due to impaired kidney function. Hyperkalemia is one such common imbalance, which can have significant effects on cardiac function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG Findings in Hyperkalemia: Tall, peaked T waves Widened QRS complexes Prolonged PR interval Flattening or absence of P waves Eventually, this can lead to a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation if untreated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tall, peaked T waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Widened QRS complexes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flattening or absence of P waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eventually, this can lead to a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation if untreated."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These ECG changes are characteristic of hyperkalemia, particularly in the setting of CKD, where the kidneys are unable to excrete potassium effectively."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"C hronic kidney disease (CKD) often leads to electrolyte imbalances due to impaired kidney function. Hyperkalemia is one such common imbalance, which can have significant effects on cardiac function."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ECG Findings in Hyperkalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tall, peaked T waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Widened QRS complexes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Flattening or absence of P waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Eventually, this can lead to a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation if untreated."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tall, peaked T waves"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Widened QRS complexes"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flattening or absence of P waves"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eventually, this can lead to a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation if untreated."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"These ECG changes are characteristic of hyperkalemia, particularly in the setting of CKD, where the kidneys are unable to excrete potassium effectively."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypokalemia: ECG findings typically include U waves, flattened or inverted T waves, and prolonged QT interval."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypercalcemia: ECG may show a shortened QT interval."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypocalcemia: ECG may show a prolonged QT interval."}],"qi":[4490]},{"n":56,"q":"A 60-year-old lady presents with SOB and angina pectoris episodes. Work up shows aortic stenosis. Which of the following is the reason behind these chest pain episodes?","o":[{"t":"Increased pressure in aorta"},{"t":"Decreased pressure in aorta"},{"t":"Increased oxygen consumption in heart"},{"t":"Increase in volume overload of heart"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Increased oxygen consumption in the heart:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aortic Stenosis and Angina: In aortic stenosis, the narrowed aortic valve obstructs blood flow from the left ventricle to the aorta. This obstruction increases the left ventricular pressure as the heart must work harder to overcome the resistance, leading to left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH). The hypertrophied myocardium increases oxygen demand but, due to the narrowed valve, coronary perfusion is compromised, especially during diastole. This imbalance between increased oxygen demand and reduced supply leads to myocardial ischemia and angina."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In aortic stenosis, the narrowed aortic valve obstructs blood flow from the left ventricle to the aorta. This obstruction increases the left ventricular pressure as the heart must work harder to overcome the resistance, leading to left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The hypertrophied myocardium increases oxygen demand but, due to the narrowed valve, coronary perfusion is compromised, especially during diastole. This imbalance between increased oxygen demand and reduced supply leads to myocardial ischemia and angina."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In aortic stenosis, the narrowed aortic valve obstructs blood flow from the left ventricle to the aorta. This obstruction increases the left ventricular pressure as the heart must work harder to overcome the resistance, leading to left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The hypertrophied myocardium increases oxygen demand but, due to the narrowed valve, coronary perfusion is compromised, especially during diastole. This imbalance between increased oxygen demand and reduced supply leads to myocardial ischemia and angina."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Increased pressure in the aorta: This is not the primary cause of angina. The pressure increases in the left ventricle, not in the aorta."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Decreased pressure in the aorta: While aortic pressure may be lower during ejection, this is not the direct cause of angina."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Increase in volume overload of the heart: Volume overload is more associated with conditions like mitral regurgitation or heart failure, not typically with the chest pain in aortic stenosis."}]},{"n":57,"q":"Patient presents with features of right heart failure and exhibits a rapid Y descent on cardiac examination. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"Pulmonary hypertension"},{"t":"Right ventricular infarction"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Constrictive Pericarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis is a condition where the pericardium becomes thickened and fibrotic, leading to impaired filling of the heart, particularly the right side. A rapid Y descent on jugular venous pressure (JVP) is a classic finding in constrictive pericarditis, reflecting rapid early diastolic filling of the ventricle due to the rigid pericardium. The patient may present with signs of right heart failure, such as peripheral edema, ascites, and hepatomegaly."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis is a condition where the pericardium becomes thickened and fibrotic, leading to impaired filling of the heart, particularly the right side. A rapid Y descent on jugular venous pressure (JVP) is a classic finding in constrictive pericarditis, reflecting rapid early diastolic filling of the ventricle due to the rigid pericardium. The patient may present with signs of right heart failure, such as peripheral edema, ascites, and hepatomegaly."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cardiac Tamponade: Cardiac tamponade typically presents with a blunted or absent Y descent on JVP tracing due to the equalization of pressures during diastole, which restricts ventricular filling. The presence of a rapid Y descent makes cardiac tamponade less likely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Pulmonary Hypertension: Pulmonary hypertension can cause right heart failure, but it is not associated with a rapid Y descent on JVP. The JVP findings in pulmonary hypertension are usually more related to elevated pressure rather than specific waveforms like a rapid Y descent."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Right Ventricular Infarction: Right ventricular infarction can present with signs of right heart failure. However, the JVP typically shows an elevated a-wave or a prominent X descent, not a rapid Y descent."}]},{"n":58,"q":"A pulmonary artery hypertension patient presented with an irregularly irregular pulse with loud P2 on auscultation. Which JVP finding will be seen in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Absent a waves"},{"t":"Cannon a waves"},{"t":"Rapid x descent"},{"t":"Rapid y descent"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has pulmonary artery hypertension (PAH) and presents with an irregularly irregular pulse and a loud P2 (second heart sound). The irregularly irregular pulse suggests the presence of atrial fibrillation, which is often associated with PAH due to right atrial enlargement from increased pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In atrial fibrillation, the irregular and often rapid contractions of the atria lead to ineffective atrial contraction. As a result, the a waves, which represent atrial contractions, are absent in the JVP waveform. This is consistent with the finding in atrial fibrillation where the rhythm is irregular and there is no coordinated atrial contraction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cannon a Waves: Cannon a waves are seen in conditions where there are atrial contractions against a closed tricuspid valve, as in cases of complete heart block or atrial fibrillation with discordance between atrial and ventricular contractions. However, this finding is not as specific as the absence of a waves in atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Rapid x Descent: Rapid x descent is more associated with conditions like constrictive pericarditis rather than atrial fibrillation, which is the primary condition described here."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rapid y Descent: Rapid y descent is indicative of constrictive pericarditis or other conditions where there is rapid filling of the ventricles. It is not typically associated with atrial fibrillation."}]},{"n":59,"q":"A young athlete underwent testing for a competition and was found to have hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Which of the following will increase the murmur in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Handgrip"},{"t":"Valsalva maneuver"},{"t":"Squatting"},{"t":"Leaning forward"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Valsalva Maneuver:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is a condition characterized by the thickening of the heart muscle, particularly the interventricular septum, which can obstruct blood flow out of the left ventricle, leading to a systolic murmur. The murmur associated with HCM is dynamic and can change in intensity with different maneuvers that affect the volume of blood in the left ventricle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Valsalva maneuver decreases venous return to the heart, leading to reduced left ventricular filling. In HCM, this reduction in ventricular volume increases the obstruction to blood flow, making the murmur louder. This maneuver accentuates the outflow tract obstruction by bringing the hypertrophied septum closer to the anterior mitral valve leaflet."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Handgrip: The handgrip maneuver increases systemic vascular resistance, which typically increases murmurs of regurgitant lesions like mitral regurgitation but decreases the murmur in HCM because the increased afterload reduces the degree of outflow tract obstruction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Squatting: Squatting increases venous return and systemic vascular resistance, leading to increased left ventricular filling and a reduction in the outflow tract obstruction. This typically softens the murmur in HCM."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Leaning Forward: Leaning forward can accentuate murmurs of aortic regurgitation but does not have a significant effect on the murmur of HCM."}]},{"n":60,"q":"Which of the following drugs will cause Torsades de pointes if given with erythromycin?","o":[{"t":"Astemizole"},{"t":"Levofloxacin"},{"t":"Amoxicillin"},{"t":"Ceftriaxone"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Astemizole:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Astemizole is an antihistamine that has been associated with QT interval prolongation, a risk factor for Torsades de pointes, a type of life-threatening ventricular tachycardia. Erythromycin, a macrolide antibiotic, is known to inhibit the cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) enzyme. Astemizole is metabolized by CYP3A4, and when given together, erythromycin can increase the levels of astemizole, leading to significant QT prolongation and a high risk of Torsades de pointes. Due to this risk, astemizole has been withdrawn from the market in many countries."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Astemizole is an antihistamine that has been associated with QT interval prolongation, a risk factor for Torsades de pointes, a type of life-threatening ventricular tachycardia. Erythromycin, a macrolide antibiotic, is known to inhibit the cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4) enzyme. Astemizole is metabolized by CYP3A4, and when given together, erythromycin can increase the levels of astemizole, leading to significant QT prolongation and a high risk of Torsades de pointes. Due to this risk, astemizole has been withdrawn from the market in many countries."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Levofloxacin: Levofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic that can also cause QT interval prolongation. However, when given with erythromycin, the risk of Torsades de pointes is lower compared to astemizole because levofloxacin is not as strongly influenced by CYP3A4 interactions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Amoxicillin: Amoxicillin is a beta-lactam antibiotic that does not prolong the QT interval and is not associated with Torsades de pointes, either alone or in combination with erythromycin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ceftriaxone: Ceftriaxone is a third-generation cephalosporin that also does not prolong the QT interval or interact with erythromycin to increase the risk of Torsades de pointes."}]},{"n":61,"q":"A patient presents with shortness of breath. Vitals are HR= 120/min, BP= 160/100. Echo shows diastolic collapse of ventricles. CT showed money bag appearance. Which is best for management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Start diuretic with BP monitoring"},{"t":"Intra aortic balloon pump"},{"t":"Pericardiocentesis"},{"t":"Ventricular assist device"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Pericardiocentesis:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis: Cardiac Tamponade"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The combination of shortness of breath, tachycardia, elevated blood pressure, diastolic collapse of the ventricles on echocardiogram, and the \"money bag\" appearance on CT strongly suggests cardiac tamponade . The \"money bag\" appearance refers to the shape of the pericardial sac when it is filled with a significant amount of fluid, leading to the characteristic external compression of the heart."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The combination of shortness of breath, tachycardia, elevated blood pressure, diastolic collapse of the ventricles on echocardiogram, and the \"money bag\" appearance on CT strongly suggests cardiac tamponade . The \"money bag\" appearance refers to the shape of the pericardial sac when it is filled with a significant amount of fluid, leading to the characteristic external compression of the heart."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management: Pericardiocentesis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade is pericardiocentesis . This procedure involves the drainage of the accumulated fluid in the pericardial sac, which relieves the pressure on the heart, allowing normal cardiac function to resume. This intervention is urgent and life-saving."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade is pericardiocentesis . This procedure involves the drainage of the accumulated fluid in the pericardial sac, which relieves the pressure on the heart, allowing normal cardiac function to resume. This intervention is urgent and life-saving."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Start diuretic with BP monitoring: Diuretics are contraindicated in cardiac tamponade as they can decrease preload, further reducing cardiac output and worsening the patient's condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Intra-aortic balloon pump: An intra-aortic balloon pump is used to support the heart in cases of cardiogenic shock, but it is not effective in cardiac tamponade, where the primary issue is external compression of the heart, not inadequate pump function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ventricular assist device: A ventricular assist device is used in severe heart failure to help the ventricles pump blood. However, it is not appropriate for cardiac tamponade, where the problem is due to fluid accumulation in the pericardium rather than intrinsic ventricular failure."}]},{"n":62,"q":"What is the next best step in management of a pulseless patient with the following rhythm?","o":[{"t":"Defibrillate and continue chest compression"},{"t":"Defibrillate and check pulse"},{"t":"Check pulse and give synchronized DC"},{"t":"Give synchronized DC and continue chest compression"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Defibrillate and continue chest compression:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulseless monomorphic ventricular tachycardia (VT) is a type of ventricular arrhythmia that requires immediate treatment. The absence of a pulse indicates that the patient is in cardiac arrest and effective treatment is critical to restore circulation and potentially save the patient's life."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For pulseless VT (or pulseless ventricular fibrillation), immediate defibrillation is indicated. Defibrillation provides an electric shock to the heart to reset the electrical activity and restore a normal rhythm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While defibrillation is being administered, chest compressions should continue as they are essential for maintaining blood flow to vital organs until normal rhythm is restored."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Defibrillate and check pulse: After defibrillation, checking for a pulse is important, but it should not delay the immediate administration of another shock if the patient remains pulseless. Compressions should continue uninterrupted during this process."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Check pulse and give synchronized DC: Checking for a pulse before defibrillation is inappropriate in the context of pulseless cardiac arrest. Synchronized direct current (DC) cardioversion is used for certain arrhythmias like atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter with a pulse, not for pulseless VT."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Give synchronized DC and continue chest compression: Synchronized DC cardioversion is not the appropriate treatment for pulseless VT. Defibrillation (not synchronized cardioversion) is required in this case."}],"qi":[4498]},{"n":63,"q":"A patient with a history of hypertension presents with atrial fibrillation, shortness of breath and Bilateral basal crackles on auscultation. Least important in management would be:","o":[{"t":"Start on anticoagulants like warf or Doac"},{"t":"Start beta blocker"},{"t":"Cardioversion to correct rhythm if hemodynamically remain unstable even after medical management"},{"t":"Iv Digoxin to control rate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Start beta blocker:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta blockers are used to control heart rate and manage symptoms related to atrial fibrillation. However, in acute settings with symptoms like shortness of breath and bilateral crackles, especially if heart failure is suspected, other treatments might take precedence. In cases of acute heart failure or unstable hemodynamics, immediate rate control might be achieved using other agents."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Beta blockers are used to control heart rate and manage symptoms related to atrial fibrillation. However, in acute settings with symptoms like shortness of breath and bilateral crackles, especially if heart failure is suspected, other treatments might take precedence. In cases of acute heart failure or unstable hemodynamics, immediate rate control might be achieved using other agents."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Start on anticoagulants like warfarin or DOAC: Anticoagulants are crucial in the management of atrial fibrillation to prevent thromboembolic events such as stroke. Given the patient's symptoms and history, anticoagulation should be considered unless contraindicated."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cardioversion to correct rhythm if hemodynamically unstable even after medical management: If the patient remains hemodynamically unstable despite medical management, cardioversion is necessary to restore normal sinus rhythm and stabilize the patient. This is crucial in managing acute atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - IV Digoxin to control rate: IV digoxin is used for rapid rate control in atrial fibrillation, especially when accompanied by heart failure. It can be beneficial in acute settings for managing symptoms and controlling the ventricular rate."}]},{"n":64,"q":"The PR interval was measured at 0.21 seconds in a routine ECG of the patient. What is the correct Interpretation?","o":[{"t":"Second degree HB"},{"t":"First degree HB"},{"t":"Normal rhythm"},{"t":"Complete HB"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - First degree HB:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-degree heart block is defined by a prolonged PR interval greater than 0.20 seconds (200 milliseconds). In this case, a PR interval of 0.21 seconds indicates a prolonged PR interval, which is characteristic of first-degree heart block."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Second degree HB: Second-degree heart block (HB) is characterized by intermittent failure of some atrial impulses to be conducted to the ventricles. This is usually identified by a pattern of dropped beats or a varying PR interval. The PR interval in second-degree HB can be normal or prolonged, but it does not specifically represent a fixed interval as seen in first-degree HB."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Normal rhythm: A normal PR interval ranges from 0.12 to 0.20 seconds. A PR interval of 0.21 seconds exceeds this range, indicating that the rhythm is not normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Complete HB: Complete heart block (also known as third-degree heart block) is characterized by a complete dissociation between atrial and ventricular activity, with no correlation between P waves and QRS complexes. The PR interval is not applicable in this context as the atrial impulses do not conduct to the ventricles."}]},{"n":65,"q":"Which of the following is associated with a Graham-Steel murmur?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular septal defect (VSD)"},{"t":"Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM)"},{"t":"Pulmonary regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Graham-Steel murmur indicates pulmonary regurgitation secondary to pulmonary hypertension. This early diastolic decrescendo murmur is heard best at the left upper sternal border (pulmonic area) and is caused by the backward flow of blood from the pulmonary artery into the right ventricle during diastole."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is specifically associated with pulmonary hypertension, which causes dilation of the pulmonary artery and subsequent incompetence of the pulmonary valve."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Murmur Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral Stenosis Low-pitched diastolic murmur with an opening snap,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"best heard at the apex"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Stenosis Crescendo-decrescendo systolic ejection murmur,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"radiates to carotids, best heard at the right upper sternal border"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA) Continuous, machine-like murmur, best heard at the left"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"infraclavicular area"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy Harsh systolic murmur with a crescendo-decrescendo (HOCM) (Option B) pattern, best heard at the left sternal border, increases"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"with Valsalva maneuver"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mitral Regurgitation (MR) Holosystolic murmur, best heard at the apex, radiates to"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"the axilla"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Aortic Regurgitation (AR) Early diastolic decrescendo murmur, best heard at the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"left sternal border"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD) Harsh holosystolic murmur, best heard at the left lower (Option A) sternal border (LLSB)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Condition Murmur Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Atrial Septal Defect (ASD) Systolic ejection murmur, best heard at the left upper"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sternal border (LUSB), with fixed, widely split S2"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tricuspid Regurgitation (TR) Holosystolic murmur, best heard at the left lower sternal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"border, increases with inspiration (Carvallo’s sign)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Murmur Cause & Characteristics"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Graham-Steel Murmur (Pulmonary Early diastolic decrescendo murmur, best heard at the Regurgitation due to Pulmonary Hypertension) left upper sternal border, caused by pulmonary valve"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"incompetence due to pulmonary hypertension"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Austin Flint Murmur Low-pitched, rumbling diastolic murmur, best heard at (Severe Aortic Regurgitation) the apex, due to turbulent flow in the left ventricle (Option D) compressing the mitral valve"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mammary Murmur (Physiologic in Pregnancy) Continuous hissing murmur, heard over the left second"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and third intercostal spaces, caused by increased blood flow through internal mammary vessels"}]},{"n":66,"q":"A 65-year-old male with chronic stable angina presents with worsening chest pain during routine activities. His current medications include aspirin, clopidogrel, metoprolol, and atorvastatin, but his symptoms persist. ECG shows ST-segment depression in V5-V6, and coronary angiography reveals 80% stenosis of the left anterior descending (LAD) artery. What is the most appropriate intervention?","o":[{"t":"Increase the dose of beta-blockers"},{"t":"Add ranolazine to medical therapy"},{"t":"Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stent (DES)"},{"t":"Refer for coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct answer: C) Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stent (DES)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has significant stenosis (80% in the LAD) and persistent anginal symptoms despite optimal medical therapy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PCI with a drug-eluting stent (DES) is the most appropriate intervention in this scenario, as it restores blood flow and relieves symptoms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DES is preferred due to its lower risk of restenosis compared to bare-metal stents."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Medical management"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Medication Class Function/Mechanism Indications Side Effects/"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Contraindication"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Nitrates Systemic venodilation Angina relief Tolerance Sublingual nitroglyc Reduces LV end-diastolic Preemptive use before development erin (0.4 or 0.6 mg) volume and pressure activities Headaches, Long-acting forms Releases nitric oxide (NO) Improves exercise dizziness (oral, transdermal) to relax vascular smooth tolerance"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"muscle"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta-Blocker Reduces heart rate, Angina relief Asthma (Option A) arterial pressure, and Reduces mortality and AV conduction"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"myocardial contractility reinfarction rates disturbances Improves survival post-MI Severe bradycar"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"dia"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Calcium Channel Coronary vasodilators Angina treatment when Bradyarrhythmia Blockers Reduces myocardial beta blockers are s"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"oxygen demand and ineffective May aggravate arterial pressure Variant angina LV failure Dihydropyridines (e.g., amlodipine) and non-dihydropyridines (e.g., verapamil)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Antiplatelet Drugs Aspirin : inhibits platelet Reduces coronary events Risk of bleeding"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"activation in high-risk populations Gastrointestinal Clopidogrel 75mg : blocks Stent placement issues, allergies platelet aggregation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other Therapies :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ranolazine : Inhibits late sodium current , minimizing Na and Ca overload in ischemic myocytes. (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SGLT2 Inhibitors : Provide cardiovascular and renal protection , promoting weight loss and reducing plasma volume; beneficial in IHD patients."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nicorandil : Opens ATP-sensitive potassium channels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ivabradine : Reduces heart rate in patients with IHD and LV dysfunction."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI) (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PCI involves balloon dilatation and stenting for symptomatic IHD with suitable stenoses in epicardial coronary arteries. Indications : Symptom-limiting angina despite optimal medical therapy. Evidence of ischemia during stress tests. Post-PCI Management : Aspirin is administered indefinitely, and P2Y12 antagonists for 1-3 months. Current-generation drug-eluting stents reduce restenosis but require prolonged dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG) (Option D)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Percutaneous Coronary Intervention (PCI) (Option C)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Involves bypassing obstructive lesions using internal mammary arteries , radial arteries , or saphenous veins . Indications : Safe with low mortality in patients without serious comorbidities and normal LV function. Effective for patients with left main coronary artery stenosis , multivessel disease , or impaired LV function . Outcomes : Angina relief in ~90% of patients after complete revascularization. Long-term survival benefits in specific populations, particularly those with diabetes or impaired LV function ."}]},{"n":67,"q":"A 72 y/o woman presents with severe chest pain and shortness of breath after a stressful argument. She is post-menopausal with no history of heart disease. O/E, she is slightly tachycardic with normal blood pressure. An echocardiogram shows left ventricular ballooning during systole, and cardiac enzymes are minimally elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Hypertensive Heart Disease"},{"t":"Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Coronary Artery Disease"},{"t":"Pulmonary Embolism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Takotsubo Cardiomyopathy"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, also known as broken heart syndrome, is characterized by transient left ventricular dysfunction often triggered by emotional or physical stress ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s recent stressful event, post-menopausal status, ballooning of the left ventricle, and minimal elevation of cardiac enzymes align perfectly with this diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy (Gebrochenes-Herz syndrome)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, also known as broken-heart"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"syndrome or stress-induced cardiomyopathy, is a non-ischemic condition primarily affecting post-menopausal women. It features temporary left ventricular dysfunction that resembles a heart attack but with minimal cardiac enzyme release and no significant obstructive coronary artery disease. The name \"Takotsubo\" is derived from Japanese, meaning octopus trap, reflecting the ballooning shape of the left ventricle during contraction."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy (Gebrochenes-Herz syndrome)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Catecholamine Hypothesis: Elevated catecholamines"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(norepinephrine, epinephrine, dopamine) indicate stress-induced sympathetic overdrive, leading to microvascular spasms and myocardial injury. Microvascular Dysfunction: Impaired function is suggested by abnormalities in coronary flow reserve and microvascular resistance. Inflammation: Imaging may show myocardial oedema, necrosis, and fibrosis, with conditions like myocarditis and autoimmune diseases potentially triggering TC. Estrogen Deficiency: Exaggerated vasoconstriction and increased sympathetic activity in post-menopausal women contribute to TC."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical features Takotsubo cardiomyopathy often mimics acute coronary"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"syndrome.(MI) Common Triggers: Emotional Stress: Events like the unexpected death of a loved one, domestic abuse, or financial loss. Physical Stress: Serious medical diagnoses or natural disasters. Symptoms: Chest pain, dyspnea, and syncope. Some patients may have signs of heart failure, such as tachyarrhythmias or bradyarrhythmias. A late-peaking systolic murmur may be present due to LVOTO. About 10% of patients may develop cardiogenic shock."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypertensive Heart Disease (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Caused by chronic high blood pressure, leading to left ventricular hypertrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient's history does not indicate long-standing hypertension."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Coronary Artery Disease (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Involves plaque buildup in coronary arteries, causing reduced blood flow."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The absence of obstructive disease on angiography and minimal enzyme release suggests this is unlikely."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary Embolism (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occurs when a blood clot blocks a pulmonary artery, leading to sudden shortness of breath and chest pain. History of long term immobilization/ DVT is present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient's findings indicate a cardiac issue, not a pulmonary one."}]},{"n":68,"q":"A 68-year-old male patient with a history of hypertension presents to the emergency department in acute distress. He is diaphoretic, tachycardic, and complains of severe chest pain. His ECG is given below. Given the patient's condition, what is the most appropriate immediate management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Radiofrequency catheter ablation"},{"t":"Digoxin"},{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Synchronized cardioversion"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's clinical presentation of acute distress, diaphoresis, tachycardia, and severe chest pain, along with the ECG showing saw-tooth flutter waves indicative of atrial flutter , suggests he is hemodynamically unstable."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This necessitates immediate synchronized cardioversion to restore normal sinus rhythm and stabilize his condition."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrial Flutter"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Features Atrial flutter is characterized by a large re-entry circuit,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"usually within the right atrium, encircling the tricuspid annulus originating from the cavotricuspid isthmus. The atrial rate is approximately 300 beats per minute . It is typically associated with 2:1, 3:1, or 4:1 AV block, resulting in ventricular rates of 150, 100, or 75 beats per minute."},{"k":"img","v":4505},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ECG Featur es"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The ECG shows saw-tooth flutter waves , often referred to as \"F-waves.\" With a 2:1 AV block, flutter waves may be buried in QRS complexes and T waves, making them difficult to identify."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis Atrial flutter should be suspected in narrow-complex"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"tachycardia at a rate of 150 beats per minute. Carotid sinus pressure or intravenous adenosine may help reveal flutter waves by temporarily increasing the degree of AV block."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Atrial Flutter"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Manageme 1. Rhythm Control: nt Acute Management:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Hemodynamically Unstable Patients: Synchronized cardioversion. Stable Patients: Electrical cardioversion (if no intracardiac thrombus). Pharmacological cardioversion: Antiarrhythmic drugs: Amiodarone Class IA: Procainamide, Quinidine, Disopyramide Class IC: Flecainide, Propafenone Calcium Channel Blockers: Verapamil, Diltiazem Beta-Blockers: Metoprolol, Carvedilol, Esmolol Long-Term Management: Radiofrequency catheter ablation of the cavotricuspid isthmus. 2. Rate Control: First-Line Agents: Calcium Channel Blockers (e.g., Verapamil, Diltiazem) Beta-Blockers (e.g., Metoprolol, Carvedilol, Esmolol) Digoxin (use cautiously due to toxicity). Goal: Heart rate < 110 beats per minute. 3. Anticoagulation: Assess stroke risk using the CHADS2-VASC score: Anticoagulation Recommendations: 2 or more points: Strong recommendation for anticoagulation. 1 point: Consider either aspirin or full anticoagulation."}],"qi":[4505]},{"n":69,"q":"A 58-year-old woman comes to the clinic for a routine follow-up. She has a history of mild hypertension, which is well-controlled with medication. She reports no symptoms such as palpitations, dizziness, or chest pain. An ECG of the patient is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the ECG findings?","o":[{"t":"Second-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"First-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"Third-Degree AV Block"},{"t":"Bundle Branch Block"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) First-Degree AV Block"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG shows a prolonged PR interval (>0.20 seconds) but consistently conducts P waves to QRS complexes, indicating first-degree AV block."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There are no dropped beats, ruling out higher-degree blocks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"First Degree AV Block"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathology First-degree AV block is characterized by a prolonged"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"PR interval (>0.20 seconds) without disruption of conduction from atria to ventricles. The delay typically occurs in the AV node, but may also involve the atria, His bundle, or Purkinje fibers."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Etiology Commonly attributed to increased vagal tone in younger"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"patients. In older patients, often due to fibrotic changes in the cardiac conduction system. Other causes include coronary heart disease, myocardial infarction, electrolyte abnormalities, inflammation, infections, certain drugs, infiltrative diseases, collagen vascular diseases, and idiopathic degenerative diseases. Lymes disease, Chagas disease, Diphtheria, Rheumatoid arthritis, Scleroderma."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Usually asymptomatic; patients are often unaware of the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"condition until discovered on an ECG. In cases where the PR interval exceeds 0.30 seconds, symptoms may resemble \"pacemaker syndrome,\" including dyspnea, malaise, lightheadedness, and chest pain."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ECG Features Diagnosis is confirmed by a PR interval greater than"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"0.20 seconds on an ECG. PR interval can be marked (>0.30 seconds) in some cases, and P waves may appear within preceding T waves."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Generally, no treatment is required; routine observation"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"is sufficient. Pacemaker placement may be considered for symptomatic patients with PR interval >0.30 seconds or those with coexisting heart disease. No antiarrhythmic medications are indicated, and surveillance with routine ECGs is recommended to monitor for worsening."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Second-Degree AV Block (Option A):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This block features progressive lengthening of the PR interval followed by a dropped QRS complex, which is absent in this ECG as there are no dropped beats."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Third-Degree AV Block (Option C):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Here, there is complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is absent in the ECG since P waves are consistently followed by QRS complexes."},{"k":"img","v":3890},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bundle Branch Block (Option D):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition shows a wide QRS complex with characteristic morphology."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This feature is absent in the ECG, where the QRS complexes are normal in width and shape"}],"qi":[3770]},{"n":70,"q":"A 25-year-old patient with caries is scheduled for a dental extraction. Which of the following cardiac conditions does not requires endocarditis prophylaxis prior to dental extraction?","o":[{"t":"Prior history of endocarditis"},{"t":"Atrial septal defect"},{"t":"Unrepaired cyanotic heart disease"},{"t":"Prosthetic heart valves"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Atrial septal defect:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with atrial septal defects do not require endocarditis prophylaxis prior to dental extraction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Prior history of endocarditis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Unrepaired cyanotic heart disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Prosthetic heart valves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with a prior history of endocarditis, unrepaired cyanotic heart disease, and prosthetic heart valves require endocarditis prophylaxis prior to dental procedures."}]},{"n":71,"q":"A patient presents to you with an irregularly irregular pulse of 120 beats/minute and a pulse deficit of 20. Which of the following would be the jugular venous pressure (JVP) finding?","o":[{"t":"Absent p wave"},{"t":"Absent a wave"},{"t":"Cannon a wave"},{"t":"Raised JVP with normal waveform"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Absent a wave:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis in this patient with an irregularly irregular pulse and an accompanying pulse deficit is atrial fibrillation. The corresponding finding on JVP examination would be ' absent a wave' due to inadequate/absent atrial contractions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Absent p wave: Absent p wave on ECG is a finding seen in patients with atrial fibrillation. It is not seen on JVP examination."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cannon a wave: Cannon a wave on JVP examination is seen in conditions such as atrioventricular dissociation and is not seen in patients with atrial fibrillation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Raised JVP with normal waveform: Raised JVP with normal waveforms may be seen in conditions such as heart failure and constrictive pericarditis and not in atrial fibrillation."}]},{"n":72,"q":"An 11-year-old child with a history of streptococcal pharyngitis presents to you with fever and arthralgia. There is no past history of rheumatic heart disease or features of carditis or valvular disease. The child weighs 25 kgs. How often is 6,00,000 IU of benzathine penicillin recommended for prophylaxis of rheumatic heart disease?","o":[{"t":"Immediately"},{"t":"Thrice weekly lifelong"},{"t":"Once in four weeks for 5 years or till the age of 21, whichever is longer"},{"t":"Once in three weeks for 10 years or till the age of 25, whichever is longer"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Once in four weeks for 5 years or till the age of 21, whichever is longer:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This patient likely has Rheumatic Fever without carditis. He fulfills 2 major criteria for the diagnosis of Rheumatic Fever fi Fever and polyarthralgia. This child should be initiated on secondary prophylaxis. Since this child weighs less than 27 kgs, the dose of benzathine penicillin G is 600,000 IU (1.2 million IU if the child weighs more than 27 kgs). The duration of prophylaxis in a patient with rheumatic fever without carditis is 'once in 3-4 weeks for 5 years after the last attack or 21 years of age whichever is longer'"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":73,"q":"A 74-year-old patient with CAD presents with palpitations. ECG is shown below. Blood pressure is 120/84 mmHg. Which of the following is the best treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Oral metoprolol"},{"t":"Oral amiodarone"},{"t":"Intravenous amiodarone"},{"t":"Intravenous metoprolol"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Intravenous amiodarone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis in this patient is stable ventricular tachycardia. The patient has a history of structural heart disease; hence, the appropriate course of management would be IV amiodarone."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4510]},{"n":74,"q":"A hypertensive patient who is non-compliant with medication presents to you with sudden onset breathlessness. A chest x-ray was done, which is shown below. How will you manage this patient?","o":[{"t":"Intravenous salbutamol"},{"t":"Intravenous nitro-glycerine"},{"t":"Nebulization with salbutamol"},{"t":"Oxygen and antibiotics"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Intravenous nitro-glycerine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The chest x-ray shows findings of pulmonary edema , which is caused by s evere left ventricular failure due to uncontrolled hypertension ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous nitro-glycerine is commonly utilized in the treatment of acute pulmonary edema caused by left ventricular failure due to uncontrolled hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nitro-glycerine is a vasodilator that works by dilating the blood vessels, decreasing the preload on the heart and this improves cardiac output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In addition, nitroglycerine dilates the pulmonary blood vessels, diminishing the resistance to the bloodstream and decreasing the amount of fluid that leaks into the lungs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C, and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4516]},{"n":75,"q":"A patient on anti-depressants presented to you with hypotension. An ECG was done, which showed wide QRS complexes and right axis deviation. How will you manage this patient?","o":[{"t":"Antiarrhythmics"},{"t":"Intravenous sodium bicarbonate"},{"t":"Propranolol"},{"t":"Wait and watch"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Intravenous sodium bicarbonate:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG discoveries of wide QRS complexes and right axis deviation is due to sodium channel blockade, which is a feature of tricyclic antidepressant toxicity."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatment is typically intravenous sodium bicarbonate, which can rectify the acidosis and turn around the impacts of the drug."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":76,"q":"Which of the following conditions is characterized by the absence of P waves?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Focal atrial tachycardia"},{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Atrial Fibrillation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The P wave on an electrocardiogram (ECG) represents the depolarization of the atria. Atrial fibrillation (AF) is a common cardiac arrhythmia characterized by a fast and irregular heartbeat originating from multiple areas in the atria. Absent P waves are a feature of AF because the atria are not depolarizing in a coordinated fashion. Instead of a regular P wave preceding each QRS complex, there are rapid, irregular fibrillatory waves with no discernible P waves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":77,"q":"Interpret the ECG given below.","o":[{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Supra ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Supra ventricular tachycardia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG in the question shows a narrow QRS complex with an increased heart rate. Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) is a type of arrhythmia that originates above the ventricles and can cause a rapid heartbeat. The ECG tracing for SVT shows a regular rhythm with a narrow QRS complex, often with a sudden onset and termination. Thus, the above ECG indicates supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Ventricular fibrillation: Ventricular fibrillation is a life-threatening arrhythmia that occurs when the ventricles (lower chambers) of the heart quiver instead of contracting properly, leading to a lack of blood flow to the body's vital organs. The ECG tracing for ventricular fibrillation shows a rapid and chaotic pattern with no identifiable P, QRS, or T waves."},{"k":"img","v":4754},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Atrial fibrillation: Atrial fibrillation is a common arrhythmia that occurs when the atria (upper chambers) of the heartbeat irregularly and often rapidly, leading to inefficient blood flow to the ventricles. The ECG tracing for atrial fibrillation shows an irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves and an irregularly spaced QRS complex"},{"k":"img","v":4759},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ventricular tachycardia: Ventricular tachycardia is a type of arrhythmia that originates in the ventricles and can lead to a fast and potentially life-threatening heart rate. The ECG tracing for ventricular tachycardia shows a regular or irregular rhythm with a wide QRS complex,often with a repeating pattern."},{"k":"img","v":4765}],"qi":[4522]},{"n":78,"q":"J-wave is seen in?","o":[{"t":"Hypothermia"},{"t":"Heat stroke"},{"t":"High-altitude pulmonary edema"},{"t":"Drowning"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypothermia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"J wave, also known as an Osborn wave, is a small hump or notch that appears on the QRS complex of an electrocardiogram (ECG) in certain conditions. It is most commonly associated with hypothermia, a condition in which the body temperature drops below normal."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The exact mechanism by which J waves develop in hypothermia is not fully understood, but it is believed to be related to changes in the electrical properties of the heart caused by cold temperatures. Specifically, hypothermia can cause a decrease in the speed of electrical conduction in the heart, which can lead to the development of the J wave."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are not associated with J waves."}]},{"n":79,"q":"A 26-year-old male patient, an accident victim started developing difficulty in breathing. On examination, his BP as 80/40 mmHg and pulses were feeble, JVP was found to be elevated with an absent Y descent, and heart sounds were muffled. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"Acute pericarditis"},{"t":"Cardiac failure"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Cardiac tamponade:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The given scenario describes a young male patient who has developed difficulty in breathing and has signs of hemodynamic compromise, including hypotension and weak pulses, elevated jugular venous pressure with absent Y descent, and muffled heart sounds. Based on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency that occurs when there is an accumulation of fluid in the pericardial sac surrounding the heart, leading to compression of the cardiac chambers and impaired filling during diastole. This can result in decreased cardiac output and hemodynamic compromise, which may manifest as hypotension, tachycardia, elevated jugular venous pressure, and muffled heart sounds. Cardiac tamponade can be caused by a variety of conditions, including trauma, infection, malignancy, and autoimmune disorders."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Constrictive pericarditis: Constrictive pericarditis is a chronic condition that results from the thickening and fibrosis of the pericardial sac, leading to impaired diastolic filling of the heart chambers. This can result in similar symptoms to those seen in cardiac tamponade, including elevated jugular venous pressure and muffled heart sounds, but the onset is typically more gradual, and the symptoms are not as severe. Constrictive pericarditis is also associated with other signs, such as Kussmaul's sign and pericardial knock."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute pericarditis: Acute pericarditis is an inflammatory condition of the pericardial sac, which can lead to chest pain, fever, and pericardial friction rub on auscultation. However, the signs and symptoms are not typically as severe as those seen in cardiac tamponade, and there is no significant hemodynamic compromise."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cardiac failure: Cardiac failure is incorrect because while cardiac tamponade can lead to heart failure, it is not the primary diagnosis in this case. Furthermore, cardiac failure typically presents with different symptoms such as shortness of breath, fatigue, and fluid accumulation in the legs."}]},{"n":80,"q":"A known hypertensive patient is brought to the ER with palpitations and breathlessness. On examination, his BP was 220/140 with signs of encephalopathy. All of the following can be used to treat this condition except?","o":[{"t":"IV Nicardipine"},{"t":"IV Mannitol"},{"t":"IV Esmolol"},{"t":"IV Sodium Nitroprusside"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - IV Mannitol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IV Mannitol does not play a role in the management of patients with hypertensive encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"caution must be exercised to avoid precipitous drops in blood pressure, which can exacerbate cerebral ischemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"V Mannitol is not typically used to lower blood pressure in hypertensive emergencies. Instead, it is used to reduce intracranial pressure in conditions such as cerebral edema or increased intracranial pressure due to trauma, tumors, or cerebral hemorrhage."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It can indirectly lower blood pressure by reducing intracranial pressure and improving cerebral perfusion, it is not a primary agent for managing hypertensive emergencies."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, C and D can be used in the treatment of hypertensive encephalopathy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IV Nicardipine, IV Esmolol, and IV Sodium Nitroprusside - are all suitable agents for rapidly lowering blood pressure in hypertensive emergencies. These medications act quickly and can be titrated to achieve the desired blood pressure reduction while minimizing the risk of complications."}]},{"n":81,"q":"A patient with chronic stable angina is having signs of heart failure. The use of which of the following drugs can decrease mortality?","o":[{"t":"Nifedipine"},{"t":"Digoxin"},{"t":"Lisinopril"},{"t":"Torsemide"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Lisinopril:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor used to treat high blood pressure and heart failure and improve survival in patients with a history of myocardial infarction. It works by blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a hormone that causes blood vessels to constrict and retain fluid, leading to increased blood pressure and fluid buildup."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Studies have shown that lisinopril improves survival in patients with heart failure and reduces the risk of hospitalization due to heart failure. It also improves symptoms such as shortness of breath, fatigue, and leg swelling."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":82,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis based on the ECG given below?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalemia"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Hypothermia"},{"t":"Hypocalcemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hypothermia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypothermia is a medical emergency that occurs when the body temperature drops below 35°C (95°F). As the body temperature drops, various physiological functions are affected, including heart function. The ECG changes seen in hypothermia are due to alterations in the electrical conduction system of the heart and are often reversible with rewarming."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG findings shown above indicate it to be hypothermia; it includes bradycardia, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, and J waves/Osborn waves. Osborn waves/J waves are positive deflections at the end of the QRS complex in the leads V3-V6 and are sometimes referred to as the \"camel hump\" sign."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hypokalemia: Hypokalemia is an electrolyte imbalance that can also affect the heart and cause ECG changes. Hypokalemia can cause flattened or inverted T waves, ST segment depression, and U waves. However, in this ECG, the findings are not consistent with either hypokalemia or hyperkalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hyperkalemia: Hyperkalemia is also an electrolyte imbalance that can affect the heart and cause ECG changes. Hyperkalemia can cause peaked T waves, widened QRS complex, and a sine wave pattern."},{"k":"img","v":4773},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypocalcemia: Hypocalcemia can also cause ECG changes, such as QT prolongation and ST segment depression. However, these changes are not seen in the given ECG, making hypocalcemia an unlikely diagnosis."}],"qi":[4529]},{"n":83,"q":"A 19-year-old woman with no comorbidities presented with numbness and paraesthesia of the fingers along with the characteristic finding shown in the image below. She has no other illnesses, and she says these episodes occur when she is under excess stress or during cold temperatures. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Primary Raynaud’s phenomenon"},{"t":"Secondary Raynaud’s phenomenon"},{"t":"Cold sores"},{"t":"Scleredema"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Primary Raynaud’s phenomenon:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary Raynaud’s phenomenon is a condition in which the blood vessels in the fingers and toes narrow in response to cold temperatures or emotional stress. As a result, the fingers and toes may feel numb or cold and may turn white or blue. This condition occurs without any underlying disease and is more common in women than men. The patient in this case has no other comorbidities and has symptoms that are typical of Primary Raynaud's phenomenon."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4535]},{"n":84,"q":"Identify the false statement regarding management of NSTEMI","o":[{"t":"Aspirin loading dose should be initated immediately"},{"t":"ACC/AHA guidelines call for either a P2Y12 inhibitor (clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor) as a class I recommendation"},{"t":"Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhibitors are useful adjuncts"},{"t":"Fibrinolytic therapy is useful in acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Fibrinolytic therapy is useful in acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fibrinolytic therapy is not recommended for NSTEMI because the underlying cause of the condition is not a complete occlusion of a coronary artery by a blood clot. Fibrinolytic therapy is typically reserved for patients with STEMI (ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction) who have evidence of a complete occlusion of a coronary artery."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Aspirin loading dose should be initiated immediately: True. Aspirin is a cornerstone of the treatment of NSTEMI, and a loading dose of 162-325 mg should be given immediately upon diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - ACC/AHA guidelines call for either a P2Y12 inhibitor (clopidogrel, prasugrel, or ticagrelor) as a class I recommendation: True. P2Y12 inhibitors are recommended as part of the standard treatment for NSTEMI, as they help to prevent platelet aggregation and reduce the risk of further cardiac events."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhibitors are useful adjuncts: True. Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhibitors are potent antiplatelet agents that can be used as adjunctive therapy in patients with NSTEMI who are at high risk for adverse cardiac events."}]},{"n":85,"q":"What is the diagnosis of a patient who exhibits dyspnea and increased distension of neck veins upon inspiration, with no murmur detected?","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"t":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Constrictive pericarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis is a condition characterised by inflammation and fibrosis of the pericardium."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This leads to stiffening of the pericardium, impairing the ability of the heart to expand and fill properly during diastole"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The key clinical findings include dyspnea and distended neck veins that increase with inspiration, known as Kussmaul's sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"During inspiration, the intrathoracic pressure decreases, causing an increase in venous return to the heart. In constrictive pericarditis, the rigid pericardium prevents the heart from expanding adequately, resulting in an exaggerated rise in jugular venous pressure. This also results in muffled heart sounds/absence of murmurs and usually shows pericardial friction rub."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Aortic regurgitation: Aortic regurgitation typically presents with a diastolic murmur , which is not mentioned in the patient's presentation. Additionally, aortic regurgitation would not cause the specific findings of distended neck veins that increase with inspiration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tricuspid stenosis: Tricuspid stenosis is associated with a diastolic murmur and symptoms such as fatigue, edema, and ascites. The key finding in tricuspid stenosis is an elevated jugular venous pressure , but it does not increase with inspiration , as seen in the patient's presentation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pulmonary arterial hypertension: Pulmonary arterial hypertension can lead to symptoms of dyspnea and distended neck veins but it does not typically cause an increase in jugular venous pressure with inspiration. It is important to note that pulmonary arterial hypertension can result from constrictive pericarditis, but it is not the primary diagnosis based solely on the given presentation."}]},{"n":86,"q":"Which medications decrease mortality in individuals suffering from heart failure?","o":[{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Furosemide"},{"t":"Prazosin"},{"t":"Torsemide"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Metoprolol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metoprolol, a beta-blocker, has been shown to lower mortality in heart failure patients. ACE inhibitors have also been shown to decrease mortality in patients with heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":87,"q":"Please determine the medical condition related to the image provided.","o":[{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral stenosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Mitral regurgitation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis, according to the image provided, is Marfan's syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marfan syndrome is an inherited autosomal dominant disorder of connective tissue with a high risk of aortic aneurysm and dissection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A common cardiovascular complication in patients with Marfan’s syndrome is mitral regurgitation due to improper connective tissue formation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4542]},{"n":88,"q":"What is the most likely condition for a male patient who has been experiencing limb weakness and sensory loss for 3 months, along with angular stomatitis? In the examination, the patient shows a lack of proprioception and vibration sensations, upper motor neuron (UMN) type weakness in the lower limbs, and no ankle reflex.","o":[{"t":"Extradural cord compression"},{"t":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"},{"t":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"t":"Subacute combined degeneration of cord"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Subacute combined degeneration of cord:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation is suggestive of Subacute combined degeneration which occurs due to the Vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the vignette, the patient has a stocking-like distribution of loss of proprioception, vibration sensations, and UMN type of lower limb weakness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"He also has angular stomatitis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Both neurological and dermatological findings are suggestive of vitamin B12 deficiency in the patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The neurological findings correspond to subacute combined degeneration of the cord which is a complication of vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Extradural cord compression: Extradural cord compression in an adult can occur due to a multitude of causes like abnormal spine alignment (scoliosis), injury to the spine, spinal tumor, rheumatoid arthritis, infection, etc. Symptoms of cord compression include burning sensation, numbness, and loss of sensation over the hand, legs, buttocks, etc, because of injury to the nerve. There is an LMN type of nerve injury because the compression causes damage to the peripheral nerves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or Lou Gehrig’s disease, is a neurological disease that causes damage to motor neurons. The patients present with a combination of signs of both LMN and UMN types of nerve damage. This patient only presents with UMN type of lesions, hence, making ALS a very unlikely cause of the patient’s symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Multiple Sclerosis: Multiple Sclerosis is an immunological disorder that occurs because of the immune system’s response against the nerves of the body. There is the destruction of the oligodendrocytes, cells responsible for the formation of the myelin sheath around the nerve, which causes exposure of the nerves. This leads to episodes of pain, loss of sensation or numbness and tingling, muscle spasms, stiffness, and weakness that remit and recur. This patient does not have a history of remission and recurrence, making MS an unlikely diagnosis."}]},{"n":89,"q":"A 40-year-old female patient presents with palpitations and difficulty in breathing. Auscultation reveals a mid-diastolic murmur and JVP shows a prominent 'a' wave. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n(or)\nProminent 'a' wave on JVP is seen in?","o":[{"t":"Mitral stenosis"},{"t":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B: - Tricuspid stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mid-diastolic murmur is seen in tricuspid stenosis and mitral stenosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prominent 'a' wave is seen in tricuspid stenosis. It is also seen in long-standing mitral stenosis, where pulmonary artery hypertension will be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Since no features of PAH are present in this patient, mitral stenosis can be ruled out,"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Mitral stenosis: Mitral stenosis causes a change in left atrial pressure. Only long-standing mitral stenosis will produce 'a' wave changes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mitral regurgitation: Mitral regurgitation causes backflow of blood from the left ventricle into the left atrium during contraction of the ventricle. There are trivial ‘v’ waves in the JVP wave due to increased left atrial compliance. There is no change in the ‘a’ waves noticed due to mitral regurgitation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Tricuspid regurgitation: Tricuspid regurgitation causes backflow of blood from the right ventricle into the right atrium during contraction of the ventricle. Tricuspid regurgitation causes obliteration of the ‘x’ wave, CV wave, and a steep 'y' descent."}]},{"n":90,"q":"In a patient who is a chronic smoker, bilateral pitting pedal edema and abdominal distension were observed. Further examination showed the presence of ascites and an S3 sound during auscultation. What are the possible defects that can be observed in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Tricuspid regurgitation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical findings of this patient, which reveal reciprocal pitting pedal edema, ascites, and an S3 on auscultation, are suggestive of right ventricular failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A history of chronic smoking likely suggests that the patient is suffering from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease leading to cor pulmonale (RV failure due to noncardiogenic causes)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased RV pressure leads to tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C, and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":91,"q":"Which of the following does not cause central cyanosis?","o":[{"t":"Methemoglobinemia"},{"t":"Pulmonary arteriovenous fistula"},{"t":"High altitude"},{"t":"Hypothermia"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Hypothermia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypothermia refers to a decrease in core body temperature below the normal range. It occurs when the body temperature drops below 95 degrees Fahrenheit (35 degrees Celsius)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypothermia causes peripheral cyanosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Central cyanosis is seen due to decreased oxygen saturation or oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Methemoglobinemia: Methemoglobin is oxidized hemoglobin due to nitrate exposure. This leads to a reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood and can result in central cyanosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pulmonary arteriovenous fistula: A pulmonary arteriovenous fistula is an abnormal connection between an artery and a vein in the lung. This can result in the bypassing of oxygenated blood from the lungs to the systemic circulation, leading to a decrease in arterial oxygen saturation and central cyanosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - High altitude: At high altitudes, the partial pressure of oxygen in the atmosphere is lower, resulting in hypobaric hypoxia. This can cause central cyanosis due to reduced oxygen availability."}]},{"n":92,"q":"Which one of the following medications is not employed for the urgent (immediate) treatment of hyperkalemia?","o":[{"t":"10% calcium gluconate over 10 min"},{"t":"Salbutamol nebulisation"},{"t":"Insulin-dextrose"},{"t":"Injection MgSO4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Injection MgSO 4 :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Magnesium sulfate (MgSO 4 ) is not typically used for the immediate management of hyperkalemia. It may be used in certain situations, such as if the patient has concurrent magnesium deficiency, which can contribute to electrolyte imbalances. However, it is not the primary drug of choice for immediate intervention in hyperkalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - 10% calcium gluconate over 10 min: Calcium gluconate is commonly used in the emergency management of hyperkalaemia. It helps stabilise the cardiac membrane and counteracts the effects of elevated potassium levels on the heart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Salbutamol nebulisation: Salbutamol, a beta-2 agonist, is frequently used for the immediate management of hyperkalaemia. It promotes the cellular uptake of potassium, effectively lowering its concentration in the blood."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Insulin-dextrose: Insulin, often administered with dextrose, is a key component of emergency hyperkalaemia management. Insulin drives potassium from the bloodstream into the cells, reducing serum potassium levels."}]},{"n":93,"q":"Identify the conduction abnormality form the ECG give below?","o":[{"t":"First degree heart block"},{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Third degree heart block"},{"t":"Second degree heart block"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Second-degree heart block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This conduction abnormality is characterized by intermittent blockage of electrical impulses between the atria and ventricles. It can be further classified into two types:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mobitz Type I (Wenckebach) - is characterized by a progressive prolongation of the PR interval until a beat is dropped"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mobitz Type II - involves intermittent blocked beats without progressive PR interval prolongation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - First-degree heart block: This conduction abnormality is characterized by a prolonged PR interval on the ECG. It indicates a delay in the electrical conduction between the atria and ventricles."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ventricular tachycardia: Ventricular tachycardia is a rapid heart rhythm originating from the ventricles. On the ECG, it appears as a wide QRS complex with a heart rate exceeding 100 beats per minute."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Third-degree heart block (complete heart block): This is a severe conduction abnormality characterised by atrioventricular dissociation."}],"qi":[4550]},{"n":94,"q":"Which of the following is a contraindication for thrombolysis?","o":[{"t":"MRI showing density in less than 1/3 rd of the area supplied by MCA"},{"t":"Blood pressure of more than 185/110 mmHg"},{"t":"Ischemic stroke within 2 hours"},{"t":"Onset of symptoms <4 hours"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Blood pressure of more than 185/110 mmHg:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thrombolysis is generally contraindicated in patients with very high blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D can be considered as indications for thrombolysis."}]},{"n":95,"q":"Which of the following is the initial treatment of choice for managing the condition in a female patient who comes to the hospital with palpitations, having stable vital signs and an ECG showing the following?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Adenosine"},{"t":"DC cardioversion"},{"t":"Primary PCI"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Adenosine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adenosine is the correct answer for first-line management of the presented condition."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG pattern shown suggests paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT), a common arrhythmia characterized by rapid heart rates originating above the ventricles. Adenosine is a potent vasodilator and affects the electrical conduction system of the heart. It activates specific receptors in the heart, particularly the adenosine A1 receptors, which help to slow down the conduction through the AV node. This interruption of the abnormal electrical pathway in SVT can restore normal sinus rhythm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4556]},{"n":96,"q":"A patient with heart disease has breathlessness on going to the bathroom. What grade does he belong to?","o":[{"t":"NYHA 3"},{"t":"NYHA 4"},{"t":"mMRC 4"},{"t":"mMRC 5"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - NYHA 3:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NYHA functional classification assesses the impact of heart disease on a patient's daily activities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"NYHA 3 indicates moderate symptoms. Patients classified as NYHA 3 experience symptoms such as marked limitation of physical activity, becoming symptomatic with less than ordinary activity (such as going to the bathroom), and having no symptoms at rest."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - NYHA 4: NYHA 4 indicates severe symptoms. Patients classified as NYHA 4 experience symptoms even at rest and are unable to carry out any physical activity without discomfort."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - mMRC 4: The mMRC dyspnea scale is used to assess dyspnea (shortness of breath) in patients with respiratory conditions. mMRC 4 indicates severe dyspnea, where patients become too breathless to leave the house or experience significant discomfort even while at rest."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - mMRC 5: mMRC 5 is not a standard category in the MMRC dyspnea scale. The scale typically ranges from 0 to 4, with 0 indicating no dyspnea and 4 indicating the most severe dyspnea."}]},{"n":97,"q":"Based on the ECG given below, which of the following is not used in the management of the condition?","o":[{"t":"Verapamil"},{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Non synchronised DC shock"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Non-synchronised DC shock:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG is suggestive of atrial fibrillation a s there is an irregularly irregular rhythm and absent P waves with narrow QRS complexes are present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In atrial fibrillation, synchronised DC shock/cardioversion can be used. Nonsynchronised DC shock (Defibrillation) is not used in the management of these patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, B and C can be used in the management of patients with atrial fibrillation."}],"qi":[4562]},{"n":98,"q":"A female patient presents to the AIIMS emergency department with severe fatigue. She is a known case of chronic stable angina. While collecting a blood sample, you notice the blood has a brownish hue. What is the underlying diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Carboxy-hemoglobinemia"},{"t":"Sideroblastic anaemia"},{"t":"Methemoglobinemia"},{"t":"Sulfhemoglobinemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Methemoglobinemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The underlying diagnosis in this case is methemoglobinemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Methemoglobinemia is a condition in which there is an increased level of methemoglobin , a form of hemoglobin that is unable to bind and transport oxygen effectively. The blood sample has a characteristic brownish hue on examination."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As the patient is known case of chronic stable angina, it is likely that she is on nitrates and methemoglobinemia is a side effect of nitrates"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":99,"q":"Which of the following are signs of heart failure? Non-pulsatile rise of JVP Orthopnoea Dyspnoea after 2 hours of sleep Right upper quadrant pain/right hypochondrial pain","o":[{"t":"1 and 2"},{"t":"1,2,3"},{"t":"2,3,4"},{"t":"1,3,4"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 2, 3, 4:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Orthopnoea is a symptom commonly seen in heart failure. It refers to difficulty breathing while lying flat and is relieved by sitting upright or propping oneself up with pillows."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dyspnoea after 2 hours of sleep: This is paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea (PND ), which is frequently associated with heart failure. PND is characterized by sudden episodes of severe shortness of breath that awaken a person from sleep . It occurs due to fluid accumulation in the lungs when lying flat during sleep."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tender hepatomegaly is a sign of right heart failure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulsatile rise of JVP is a sign of heart failure"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non pulsatile rise of JVP is seen in cardiac tamponade in which raised right ventricular and atrial pressure are present. Hence this statement is false/incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer."}]},{"n":100,"q":"What is the correct order for cardiac auscultation, from superior to inferior? a. Pulmonary b. Tricuspid c. Mitral","o":[{"t":"a > b > c"},{"t":"b > a > c"},{"t":"c > a > b"},{"t":"c > b > a"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - a > b > c:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct order for auscultation of the heart sound from superior to inferior is pulmonary > tricuspid > mitral."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":101,"q":"What is the ECG depicted in the following image?","o":[{"t":"Pericarditis"},{"t":"Viral myocarditis"},{"t":"Torsades de pointes"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Torsades de pointes:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this ECG, there is a gradual change in QRS amplitude, twisting around the baseline and giving a \"twisting\" appearance. The QRS complexes may appear to \"morph\" or change in amplitude and direction. The QRS complexes may be wide and irregular, varying in amplitude and shape. Torsades de pointes is associated with a prolonged QT interval, often greater than 500 ms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Torsades de Pointes is a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia marked by QRS complex twisting around an isoelectric line on ECG, often associated with QTc prolongation (>450 ms in males, >460 ms in females). QTc >500 ms correlates with 2-3 fold higher risk. It may spontaneously resolve or degenerate into ventricular fibrillation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes include congenital or acquired QTc prolongation, often drug-related. Risk factors include older age (>65), female gender, electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia), bradycardia, heart disease, and diuretic use. Genetic variants are Romano-Ward and Jervell and Lange-Nielsen syndromes, the latter involving congenital deafness."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect and are not associated with the findings shown in the image below."}],"qi":[4569]},{"n":102,"q":"Which of the following describes aortic regurgitation murmur?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular contraction"},{"t":"Ejection systolic murmur"},{"t":"Diastolic murmur"},{"t":"Systolic murmur"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Diastolic Murmur:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aortic regurgitation is a valvular disorder where there is abnormal leakage or backward flow of blood from the aorta into the left ventricle during diastole (relaxation phase of the cardiac cycle). This causes a characteristic murmur that is heard during diastole."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":103,"q":"A 20-year-old woman presents with breathlessness and chest pain. She is a known case of mitral stenosis. Her pulse is irregularly irregular. No thrombus is seen on echocardiography. What is the best agent to prevent future thrombotic events?","o":[{"t":"Dabigatran"},{"t":"Aspirin 150mg"},{"t":"Oral warfarin"},{"t":"Aspirin + Clopidogrel"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Oral warfarin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral warfarin is the best agent to prevent future thrombotic events in this patient with mitral stenosis. It addresses the underlying risk of thrombus formation in the left atrium associated with the irregularly irregular pulse seen in atrial fibrillation, which commonly coexists with mitral stenosis. Regular monitoring of the INR is necessary to maintain the therapeutic range and minimize bleeding risk."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":104,"q":"Evaluation of a patient revealed the presence of mild diastolic murmur. JVP showed prominent a-waves. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Mitral stenosis"},{"t":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"},{"t":"Tricuspid regurgitation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Tricuspid Stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tricuspid stenosis is the likely diagnosis based on the mild diastolic murmur and the presence of prominent a-waves on the JVP. These findings are consistent with the narrowing of the tricuspid valve and the resulting elevated right atrial pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Due to the narrow opening, when blood passes from Right Atrium to the right ventricle, turbulence is formed leading to the formation of mild diastolic murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are not associated with this clinical presentation."}]},{"n":105,"q":"A 45 year old woman with a history of hypertension presented to the emergency room with loss of consciousness, chest pain, and diaphoresis. On examination, she was unstable, and the bilateral pulses were unequal. ECG showed nonspecific ST-T changes. Which of the following is particularly useful in evaluating the function and integrity of cardiac valves?","o":[{"t":"MRI"},{"t":"Transoesophageal echocardiography"},{"t":"Cardiac enzymes"},{"t":"X-ray"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Transoesophageal echocardiography:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The likely diagnosis in this patient is aortic dissection and the most appropriate investigation out of these options would be transesophageal echocardiography."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Transoesophageal echocardiography is a diagnostic procedure that involves inserting a specialized probe into the esophagus to obtain high-resolution images of the heart and its structures. It allows for a detailed evaluation of cardiac function, valve function, and potential sources of cardiac pathology, such as blood clots, dissections, or structural abnormalities."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging): MRI is a powerful imaging modality that provides detailed images of the body's structures. However, in an acute setting where a patient is unstable with symptoms of loss of consciousness, chest pain, and diaphoresis, MRI is not the most appropriate initial investigation. MRI scans take time to perform and may not be readily available in emergency situations. Additionally, in this case, the patient's condition suggests a potential acute cardiovascular event, for which other investigations would be more suitable."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cardiac enzymes: Cardiac enzymes, such as troponin and creatine kinase-MB (CK-MB), are blood tests that assess myocardial damage. Elevated levels of these enzymes indicate injury or infarction of the heart muscle, which can occur in conditions like a heart attack. While cardiac enzymes are essential in the evaluation of chest pain and suspected myocardial injury, they do not provide immediate information about the current hemodynamic instability and the cause of the patient's symptoms. Therefore, in this acute and unstable scenario, the priority lies in assessing the cardiac structures, which can be achieved through TOE."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - X-ray: X-ray imaging can be useful for evaluating certain conditions, such as lung pathology or signs of heart enlargement. However, it is not the most appropriate investigation in this case where the patient's presentation suggests an acute cardiovascular event."}]},{"n":106,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a male patient who presents with weak femoral pulses and an upper-limb blood pressure reading of 186/90 mmHg, along with an enlarged intercostal arteries seen on a chest x-ray?","o":[{"t":"Coarctation of aorta"},{"t":"Atrial septal defect"},{"t":"Bicuspid aortic valve"},{"t":"Patent ductus arteriosus"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Coarctation of the aorta:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coarctation of the aorta is a congenital heart defect characterized by a narrowing of the aorta, typically occurring after the branching of the left subclavian artery. This narrowing obstructs blood flow and leads to increased blood pressure in the upper extremities and decreased blood pressure in the lower extremities. The feeble femoral pulses and elevated upper-limb blood pressure (186/90 mmHg) seen in the patient are consistent with the findings seen in coarctation of the aorta. Enlarged intercostal arteries on the chest X-ray can also be seen as collateral circulation develops to bypass the aortic obstruction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":107,"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with a history of recurrent retrosternal chest pain episodes at rest lasting about 5 min and partially subsiding with sublingual nitrate. ECG shows left ventricular hypertrophy and flat T-wave. He is a known case of hypertension, diabetes mellitus, and hypercholesterolemia currently on aspirin, atenolol, metformin, and lovastatin. What is the next best step in management?","o":[{"t":"IV glyceryl trinitrate infusion"},{"t":"Injection enoxaparin"},{"t":"Add clopidogrel"},{"t":"Increase the dose of beta blocker"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Injection enoxaparin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is the correct answer. The patient's clinical presentation, history of recurrent chest pain, and ECG findings of left ventricular hypertrophy and flat T-waves raise concerns for unstable angina or acute coronary syndrome. Injection enoxaparin, a low molecular weight heparin, is often used as an initial treatment to prevent clot formation and progression of coronary artery disease. It is an appropriate choice in this setting to reduce the risk of thrombotic complications."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Option A, C and D are not appropriate next steps in this patient with unstable angina."}]},{"n":108,"q":"What condition is linked to Dilated Cardiomyopathy?","o":[{"t":"Diphtheria"},{"t":"Syphilis"},{"t":"Meningococcus"},{"t":"Brucellosis"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Diphtheria:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"One of the common complications of diphtheria is the inflammation of heart muscles or myocarditis. This can eventually result in cardiomyopathies."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are not characteristically associated with cardiomyopathies."}]},{"n":109,"q":"PR segment in ECG indicates","o":[{"t":"Atrial depolarization"},{"t":"Atrioventricular nodal conduction"},{"t":"Ventricular depolarization"},{"t":"Ventricular repolarisation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Atrioventricular nodal conduction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The PR segment indicates atrioventricular nodal conduction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - P wave indicates atrial depolarization in the ECG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - The QRS complex indicates ventricular depolarization."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - The T wave represents ventricular repolarization in the ECG."}]},{"n":110,"q":"Which heart sound is always pathological?","o":[{"t":"S1"},{"t":"S2"},{"t":"S3"},{"t":"S4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - S4:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S4 heart sound is always pathological ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An S4 heart sound is often a sign of diastolic heart failure or active ischemia. It is rarely a normal finding, unlike an S3."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In individuals with active myocardial ischemia, adequate adenosine triphosphate cannot be synthesized to release myosin from actin. The myocardium will not be able to relax as a result of this. In such conditions, S4 will be present."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a patient experiencing atrial fibrillation, the S4 heart sound will not be heard if the atria do not contract."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The S4 sound is low-pitched. It is best heard at the apex with the patient in the left lateral decubitus position."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":111,"q":"In which of the following conditions is water hammer pulse observed?","o":[{"t":"Aortic stenosis"},{"t":"Aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis and aortic regurgitation"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Aortic regurgitation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Water hammer pulse, also known as Corrigan's pulse or pistol-shot pulse, refers to a forceful and rapidly collapsing arterial pulse. It is commonly associated with aortic regurgitation, a condition where the aortic valve fails to close properly, leading to backflow of blood from the aorta into the left ventricle during diastole."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":112,"q":"What is the likely diagnosis for a 70-year-old man who has a medical history of diabetes mellitus (DM) and hypertension (HTN), takes regular medication, and is now experiencing increasing difficulty breathing during physical activity? Upon examination, the patient has an elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP), positive hepatojugular reflux, crackling sounds in the lower lungs, an enlarged liver, fluid buildup in the abdomen (ascites), and swelling in the feet (pedal edema).","o":[{"t":"Heart failure"},{"t":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Mitral regurgitation"},{"t":"Portal hypertension"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Heart failure:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the correct answer. The patient's symptoms, examination findings, and medical history are consistent with heart failure. The presence of dyspnea on exertion, elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP), positive hepatojugular reflux, crackles at the lung bases, hepatomegaly, ascites, and pedal edema are classic signs of congestive heart failure. The patient's history of diabetes mellitus (DM) and hypertension (HTN) are common risk factors for the development of heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":113,"q":"Which one of the following is not an identifiable radiological observation in a patient experiencing left heart failure?","o":[{"t":"Kerley b lines"},{"t":"Focal oligemia"},{"t":"Increased venous blood in lung"},{"t":"Change in upper lobe circulation"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Focal oligemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Focal oligemia is not a typical radiological finding in a patient with left heart failure. Focal oligemia refers to decreased blood flow to a specific area of the lung. While decreased blood flow can occur in various lung conditions, it is not a specific radiological finding associated with left heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Kerley B lines: Kerley B lines are thin linear opacities seen peripherally in the lung fields on a chest X-ray or CT scan. They represent interstitial edema and are commonly associated with left heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Increased venous blood in lung: Left heart failure can lead to increased pressure in the pulmonary veins, causing congestion and the accumulation of venous blood in the lung. This can be visualized on imaging studies such as chest X-rays as increased vascular markings and diffuse opacities in the lung fields."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Change in upper lobe circulation: In left heart failure, there can be redistribution of blood flow within the lung, leading to changes in the upper lobe circulation. This may manifest as increased blood flow to the upper lobes and decreased blood flow to the lower lobes on imaging studies."}]},{"n":114,"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with an episode of acute onset dizziness and loss of consciousness which lasted for a few seconds followed by a regain of full consciousness. There were no similar episodes in the past. Which of the following is a true statement regarding this scenario?","o":[{"t":"ECG to rule out atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"If ECG is normal, CT scan should be done"},{"t":"Tilt-table testing"},{"t":"Vestibular neuritis is a possible condition that can cause these symptoms"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - ECG to rule out atrial fibrillation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, the patient experienced a brief episode of dizziness and loss of consciousness, followed by a rapid return to full consciousness. This suggests a transient episode of syncope (temporary loss of consciousness) or near-syncope. Syncope can have various causes, including cardiac, neurological, and vasovagal etiologies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Performing an ECG (electrocardiogram) is an appropriate initial step to evaluate this patient. Atrial fibrillation (a type of abnormal heart rhythm) can cause episodes of syncope, particularly if it leads to significant changes in heart rate or rhythm. Therefore, ruling out atrial fibrillation is an important consideration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - If ECG is normal, CT scan should be done: If the ECG is normal, it does not necessarily exclude other potential causes of syncope. Further evaluation may still be required. CT scan is not typically the initial investigation for transient episodes of syncope unless there are specific indications based on the patient's history or examination findings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tilt-table testing: Tilt-table testing is generally reserved for patients with recurrent or unexplained syncope and is not typically indicated for a single episode with no previous similar events."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Vestibular neuritis is a possible condition that can cause these symptoms: While vestibular neuritis can cause dizziness, it does not typically cause loss of consciousness. It is not the most likely diagnosis in this case."}]},{"n":115,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for an overweight elderly man who arrived at the emergency department with intense chest pain lasting for two hours? He also experienced excessive sweating, pain in the left arm, nausea, and one instance of vomiting. During examination, his pulse rate was recorded as 58 beats per minute and his blood pressure was 90/60 mm of Hg. An electrocardiogram (ECG) was conducted and revealed the following results. Additionally, the patient's cardiac biomarkers indicated increased troponin levels.","o":[{"t":"Anterior wall"},{"t":"Inferior wall MI"},{"t":"Posterior wall MI"},{"t":"Pericarditis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Inferior wall MI:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis of an Inferior Wall myocardial infarction is made by elevated cardiac biomarkers (Trop I, CPK MB, LDH ), the ECG changes of myocardial Ischemia and the clinical symptoms (Levine sign) such as profuse sweating pain radiating to the left and nausea and vomiting. Here the ECG depicts evident ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Anterior wall: In Anterior Wall M I, ECG changes in V1 to V4, aVL are seen."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Posterior wall MI: Posterior Wall MI, V1 to V4 ST segment involvement is seen."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pericarditis: Pericarditis presents clinically with diffuse chest pain. Patient ECG reveals PR segment depression and ST-segment elevation (saddle-like) in all leads except aVR."}],"qi":[4579]},{"n":116,"q":"What is the diagnosis if the axis is on the mark “?” from the diagram shown below?","o":[{"t":"Right axis deviation"},{"t":"Normal axis"},{"t":"Left axis deviation"},{"t":"Extreme axis deviation"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A- Right Axis Deviation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The area marked with \"?\" lies in the range of +90° to +180° , which indicates right axis deviation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normal Axis : The normal QRS axis typically ranges from -30° to +90° ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right Axis Deviation (RAD) : QRS axis greater than +90°."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Left Axis Deviation (LAD) : QRS axis less than -30°."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Extreme Axis Deviation : Between +180° and -90°."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Since the cardiac axis in the diagram is between +90° and +180° , which represents right axis deviation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect, as explained."}],"qi":[4585]},{"n":117,"q":"Please determine the rhythm displayed in the provided ECG rhythm strip.","o":[{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial flutter"},{"t":"Normal sinus rhythm"},{"t":"AV block"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Normal sinus rhythm:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above ECG strip is that of a normal sinus rhythm. The QRS complexes are narrow, each QRS complex is preceded by a P wave, the PR interval is constant and the RR interval is constant."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4591]},{"n":118,"q":"An intern observed that a patient was unresponsive, and an ECG which was performed urgently showed an arrhythmia as below. DC shock was delivered but the arrhythmia persisted, and the patient is still unresponsive. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"},{"t":"Start CPR"},{"t":"Deliver another DC shock"},{"t":"1 mg epinephrine IV"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Start CPR:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Starting CPR is the next best step ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ECG reveals ventricular fibrillation which is a life-threatening arrhythmia having high-frequency ventricular contraction resulting in decreased cardiac output and hemodynamic collapse. Since one cycle of defibrillation is already given and the patient is still unresponsive , CPR has to be given for 2 minutes, after which the rhythm is to be assessed again."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4594]},{"n":119,"q":"Tall and tented T waves, wide QRS, and absent P wave are features of:","o":[{"t":"Bundle branch block"},{"t":"Acute MI"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Ventricular ectopic"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hyperkalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperkalemia is characterized by increased serum potassium levels. ECG reveals tall tented T waves with ST elevation with broad QRS complex and absent p waves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":120,"q":"In order to diagnose resistant hypertension, the patient must have uncontrolled high blood pressure even after being treated with three different antihypertensive medications. One of these medications should be:","o":[{"t":"Calcium channel blocker"},{"t":"Beta-blocker"},{"t":"Alpha blocker"},{"t":"Diuretic"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Diuretic:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Resistant hypertension is defined as uncontrolled blood pressure despite using three different antihypertensive medications from different drug classes, including a diuretic. The diuretic is an essential component in treating hypertension as it helps reduce fluid volume and decrease blood pressure. It is recommended that at least one of the three antihypertensive drugs used in the treatment of resistant hypertension should be a diuretic."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":121,"q":"Which of the following foods is not included in the DASH diet?","o":[{"t":"Rich in potassium"},{"t":"Rich in calcium"},{"t":"Rich in sodium"},{"t":"Rich in magnesium"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Rich in sodium:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The DASH diet recommends limiting the intake of sodium, as high sodium consumption is associated with increased blood pressure. The diet suggests reducing the use of table salt and avoiding processed foods that are typically high in sodium, such as fast food, canned soups, and salty snacks."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Rich in potassium: The DASH diet encourages foods that are rich in potassium, such as fruits (e.g., bananas, oranges), vegetables (e.g., spinach, broccoli), and legumes (e.g., beans, lentils). Potassium is known to help lower blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Rich in calcium: The DASH diet also includes foods that are rich in calcium, such as low-fat dairy products (e.g., milk, yogurt, cheese) and calcium-fortified foods (e.g., tofu, cereals). Calcium is important for maintaining strong bones and teeth."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Rich in magnesium: The DASH diet promotes foods that are rich in magnesium, such as whole grains (e.g., brown rice, whole wheat bread), nuts, seeds, and green leafy vegetables. Magnesium is beneficial for various body functions, including maintaining normal blood pressure."}]},{"n":122,"q":"A 68-year-old male presents with palpitations and shortness of breath. His medical history includes hypertension, diabetes, and coronary artery disease. On examination, irregularly irregular heartbeats are noted. An electrocardiogram (ECG) reveals irregularly irregular rhythm, no P waves, and absence of an isoelectric baseline. His blood pressure is 132/84 mmHg, heart rate 130 bpm, and oxygen saturation 94% on room air. Given the patient's clinical presentation and history, which of the following treatment options should be considered as a first-line approach for managing this case?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Electrical cardioversion"},{"t":"Direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC) therapy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Metoprolol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the patient's age, comorbidities (hypertension, diabetes, coronary artery disease), and irregularly irregular heartbeats are indicative of atrial fibrillation (AF). The first step in managing AF is often to control the heart rate, especially in cases where the ventricular rate is elevated and causing symptoms like palpitations and shortness of breath. Beta-blockers, like metoprolol, are commonly used for rate control in AF. They slow down the heart rate and improve symptoms by reducing the frequency of ventricular response to the irregular atrial activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Amiodarone: While amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic medication that can be used to restore normal sinus rhythm, it is typically reserved for rhythm control strategies and not the first-line approach in managing rate control in AF."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Electrical cardioversion: Cardioversion might be considered for patients with severe symptoms or hemodynamic instability, but it's not usually the initial step unless urgent intervention is required."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC) therapy: While anticoagulation is important in patients with AF to reduce the risk of thromboembolic events, initiating anticoagulation is not the primary step when dealing with the patient's palpitations and shortness of breath. Rate controland symptom management take precedence in this acute scenario."}]},{"n":123,"q":"Which of the following statements about heart failure is false? HFpEF refers to heart failure in which the ejection fraction is preserved, while HFrEF refers to heart failure with a reduced ejection fraction.","o":[{"t":"Regardless of ejection fraction and the type of heart failure, the 5-year mortality rate is around 50%"},{"t":"Non-cardiovascular death is more in HFpEF-30-40% as compared to HFrEF-15%"},{"t":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors cause cough in 15% and angioedema in 1%"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation is common in the elderly and is the cause of stroke in a quarter of the stroke cases"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Non-cardiovascular death is more in HFpEF-30-40% as compared to HFrEF-15%:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF) is associated with a higher rate of non-cardiovascular deaths compared to heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF). In HFrEF, approximately deaths are attributed to non-cardiovascular causes, such as infections or other comorbidities. In contrast, HFpEF tends to have a lower proportion of non-cardiovascular deaths, estimated to be around 15%. Therefore this statement is false."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Regardless of ejection fraction and the type of heart failure, the 5-year mortality rate is around 50%: This statement is generally true. Heart failure, whether with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) or reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), is associated with a significant risk of mortality. The 5-year mortality rate for heart failure is indeed high, estimated to be around 50%, regardless of the specific type or ejection fraction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Angiotensin converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitors cause cough in 15% and angioedema in 1%: This statement is generally true. ACE inhibitors, commonly prescribed medications for heart failure management, can cause side effects such as cough and angioedema. Approximately 15% of patients taking ACE inhibitors may experience a dry, persistent cough."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Atrial fibrillation is common in the elderly and is the cause of stroke in a quarter of stroke cases: This statement is generally true. Atrial fibrillation (AF) is a common cardiac arrhythmia, particularly in the elderly population. It is estimated that AF is present in a significant proportion of elderly individuals. AF is associated with an increased risk of stroke, and it is estimated to be the cause of stroke in approximately one-quarter of all stroke cases."}]},{"n":124,"q":"A patient has presented with features of cardiac tamponade. Which of the following is correct regarding this condition?","o":[{"t":"Pulsus Paradoxsus is always present"},{"t":"Kussmaul sign is always present"},{"t":"Tall A wave is present"},{"t":"Jugular veins column is visibly distended without pulsations"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Jugular veins column is visibly distended without pulsations:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac tamponade refers to the accumulation of fluid in the pericardial sac, which puts pressure on the heart and impairs its ability to pump effectively."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Jugular veins column is visibly distended without pulsations: This is a characteristic finding in cardiac tamponade. Due to the increased pressure in the pericardial sac, the jugular veins become visibly distended, but the normal pulsations with each heartbeat are diminished or absent."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pulsus Paradoxus is always present: Pulsus paradoxus is a finding characterized by an abnormal drop in blood pressure during inspiration. While it can be seen in cardiac tamponade, it is not always present and can vary depending on the severity of tamponade and other factors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Kussmaul sign is always present: Kussmaul sign refers to an abnormal rise in jugular venous pressure during inspiration. It is not specific to cardiac tamponade and can be seen in various cardiac and respiratory conditions. It is not always present in cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Tall A wave is present: The A wave represents atrial contraction on the jugular venous pulse waveform. In cardiac tamponade, the elevated pressure in the pericardial sac can impair atrial filling, leading to a reduced or absent A wave rather than a tall A wave."}]},{"n":125,"q":"What is the clinical diagnosis for a young male who has no symptoms but is found to have a wide fixed split S2 during auscultation?","o":[{"t":"ASD"},{"t":"VSD"},{"t":"TOF"},{"t":"BAV"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - ASD:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An atrial septal defect is a congenital heart defect characterized by an abnormal opening in the septum (the wall) between the two atria of the heart. A wide fixed split S2 on auscultation is a characteristic finding in atrial septal defects (ASD), where there is abnormal communication between the atria. It allows increased blood flow from the left atrium to the right atrium, causing a delay in the closure of the pulmonic valve, resulting in a wide fixed split S2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - VSD (Ventricular Septal Defect): A ventricular septal defect is another type of congenital heart defect where there is an abnormal opening in the septum between the two ventricles. It typically produces a harsh holosystolic murmur, rather than a wide fixed split S2 heard on auscultation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - TOF (Tetralogy of Fallot): Tetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart condition characterized by a combination of four heart defects: ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and overriding aorta. The characteristic murmur in TOF is a systolic ejection murmur and not specifically associated with a wide fixed split S2."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - BAV (Bicuspid Aortic Valve): Bicuspid aortic valve is a congenital heart condition in which the aortic valve has two cusps instead of the normal three. While it can be associated with aortic stenosis or regurgitation, it is not specifically associated with a wide fixed split S2."}]},{"n":126,"q":"A chronic smoker presented with shortness of breath, bilateral pitting pedal edema, and abdominal distension. On examination, jugular venous pulse was found to be elevated and liver was palpable 8 cm below the costal margin. An abnormality in which of the following structures is responsible for the patient’s symptoms?","o":[{"t":"1"},{"t":"2"},{"t":"3"},{"t":"4"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - 4:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The combination of shortness of breath, bilateral pitting pedal edema, elevated jugular venous pulse, and hepatomegaly suggests right sided congestive heart failure (CHF)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"4 th arrow points at the right ventricle the likely affected chamber in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - 1: The first points at the left atrium, but the diagnosis here is that of a patient with right sided heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B -2: The second arrow points at the left ventricle. Therefore this option is also incorrect."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C -3: The third arrow points toward the right atrium."}],"qi":[4602]},{"n":127,"q":"A 47 year old woman was brought to the casualty in an unconscious state after having suddenly collapsed. As there was no pulse, cardiopulmonary resuscitation was started and the monitor showed the following rhythm. What is the next step?","o":[{"t":"Defibrillation"},{"t":"Synchronized cardioversion"},{"t":"1mg epinephrine"},{"t":"0.5mg atropine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Defibrillation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the information provided and the rhythm strip, the likely diagnosis is ventricular fibrillation. Out of the given options, the most appropriate next step in management would be unsynchronized cardioversion/defibrillation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect"}],"qi":[4605]},{"n":128,"q":"Pulsus paradoxus is seen in:","o":[{"t":"Coronary artery disease"},{"t":"Severe Asthma or Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease"},{"t":"Atrial Fibrillation"},{"t":"Right ventricular myocardial infarction"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Severe Asthma or Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"During severe asthma attacks or exacerbations of COPD, airway resistance increases significantly during inspiration. This leads to increased negative intrathoracic pressure, affecting venous return to the heart and causing a drop in systolic blood pressure during inspiration."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are not associated with pulsus paradoxus."}]},{"n":129,"q":"Takayasu arteritis most commonly involves:","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary artery"},{"t":"Coronary arteries"},{"t":"Abdominal aorta"},{"t":"Arch of aorta"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Arch of aorta:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Takayasu arteritis typically affects the aorta and its major branches, particularly the branches arising from the arch of the aorta. The disease leads to inflammation, thickening, and narrowing of these arteries, which can result in reduced blood flow to various organs and tissues. The most commonly involved area in Takayasu arteritis is the aorta, specifically the branches arising from the aortic arch, which includes the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pulmonary artery: The involvement of the pulmonary artery, coronary arteries, and abdominal aorta can occur in Takayasu arteritis, but it is less common compared to the involvement of the arch of the aorta. Pulmonary artery involvement, known as pulmonary arteryarteritis, occurs in a small proportion of cases. It can lead to pulmonary hypertension and otherpulmonary complications."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Coronary arteries: Coronary artery involvement, known as coronary arteritis, can occur in Takayasu arteritis but is relatively rare. It can result in reduced blood flow to the heart, leading to symptoms such as chest pain (angina) or even heart attack."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Abdominal aorta: Abdominal aorta involvement can also be seen in Takayasu arteritis, but it is less common compared to the involvement of the arch of the aorta"}]},{"n":130,"q":"A 64-year-old man presents with complaints of palpitations and syncope. He has a history of inferior-wall myocardial infarction a year ago. Holter study was done and his 24 hour ECG strip obtained is given below. What is the long-term treatment for this condition?","o":[{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Atropine only"},{"t":"Permanent pacemaker"},{"t":"Atropine and isoproterenol"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Permanent pacemaker:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given information, the correct long-term treatment option for the condition described would be a \"Permanent pacemaker.\" The 24-hour ECG strip suggests the presence of sinus node dysfunction (sick sinus syndrome) characterized by bradycardia (slow heart rate), pauses, and sinus arrests. This condition is commonly seen in individuals who have had a previous myocardial infarction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Amiodarone: Amiodarone is an antiarrhythmic medication used to treat various cardiac arrhythmias. However, it is not the primary treatment for sinus node dysfunction. In this case, the patient's symptoms are related to bradycardia (slow heart rate) rather than a specific arrhythmia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Atropine only: Atropine is a medication used for acute management of symptomatic bradycardia. However, in the long term, it is not a suitable treatment for sinus node dysfunction. Atropine is typically used in emergency situations or as a temporary measure before definitive treatment such as a pacemaker is implemented."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Atropine and isoproterenol: Isoproterenol is a medication that acts as a cardiac stimulant and can increase heart rate. While it may be used in certain acute situations, it is not an appropriate long-term treatment for sinus node dysfunction. Additionally, combining atropine and isoproterenol is not a typical approach for managing this condition."}],"qi":[4609]},{"n":131,"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with recurrent episodes of chest pain which is seen only on exertion or exercise and lasts 2-5 minutes. He was assessed and was diagnosed with a case of stable angina however his BP was consistently elevated.\nWhat is the most appropriate antihypertensive for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Atenolol"},{"t":"Thiazides"},{"t":"Enalapril"},{"t":"Amlodipine"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Amlodipine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most appropriate antihypertensive for a patient with stable angina and consistently elevated blood pressure would be Amlodipine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Amlodipine is a calcium channel blocker that is frequently used to treat hypertension. It is particularly beneficial in patients with stable angina, as it can improve coronary blood flow by reducing vasospasm and decreasing myocardial oxygen demand. Additionally, amlodipine has a favorable effect on blood pressure control, which makes it suitable for patients with both stable angina and hypertension."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, based on the patient's diagnosis of stable angina and consistently elevated blood pressure, the most appropriate antihypertensive choice would be Amlodipine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stable angina is a condition characterized by chest pain or discomfort that occurs during physical exertion or stress and is relieved by rest or nitroglycerin. It is commonly associated with underlying coronary artery disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the patient has stable angina and consistently elevated blood pressure. Treating hypertension is an essential component of managing stable angina, as uncontrolled high blood pressure can worsen ischemic symptoms and increase the risk of cardiovascular events."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Atenolol: Atenolol is a beta-blocker commonly used to treat hypertension. While beta-blockers can be effective in managing stable angina by reducing heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand, they are not the ideal choice in patients with consistently elevated blood pressure, as they may further worsen blood pressure control."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Thiazides: Thiazide diuretics are a class of medications used to treat hypertension. They help reduce blood pressure by promoting diuresis and decreasing fluid volume. However, they may not be the most appropriate choice in this case since the patient already has stable angina, and thiazides are not specifically indicated for the treatment of angina."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Enalapril: Enalapril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor that is commonly used in the management of hypertension. While ACE inhibitors are effective antihypertensive agents, they may not be the initial choice in this case, considering the patient's primary symptom of stable angina. Other factors, such as heart rate control and relief of ischemic symptoms, need to be considered."}]},{"n":132,"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate position for placement of pacemaker leads?","o":[{"t":"Right ventricle"},{"t":"Left atrium"},{"t":"Left ventricle"},{"t":"Superior vena cava"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Right ventricle:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The pacemaker lead is typically placed in the right ventricle for most pacemaker implantations. This positioning allows the electrical impulses generated by the pacemaker to be delivered to the right ventricle, which then stimulates the heart muscle to contract and maintain an appropriate heart rhythm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":133,"q":"A postoperative cardiac surgery patient developed sudden hypotension, raised central venous pressure, pulsus paradoxus at the 4th post operative hour. The most probable diagnosis is.","o":[{"t":"Ventricular dysfunction"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"CHF"},{"t":"Excessive mediastinal effusion"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Cardiac tamponade:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the symptoms described in the postoperative cardiac surgical patient, the most probable diagnosis is cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency characterized by the accumulation of fluid or blood in the pericardial sac, which leads to compression of the heart and impaired cardiac function. The symptoms typically include sudden hypotension (low blood pressure), raised central venous pressure (elevated jugular venous distention), and pulsus paradoxus (an exaggerated drop in blood pressure during inspiration). These symptoms usually occur within the first few hours to days after surgery."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Ventricular dysfunction: While ventricular dysfunction can cause hypotension and cardiac symptoms, it does not typically present with raised central venous pressure or pulsus paradoxus. Additionally, the sudden onset of symptoms in this case suggests a more acute problem like cardiac tamponade rather than chronic ventricular dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - CHF (Congestive Heart Failure): CHF can cause fluid retention, leading to increased venous pressure and symptoms such as dyspnea and edema. However, it does not typically present with sudden hypotension, raised central venous pressure, or pulsus paradoxus. Cardiac tamponade is a more likely explanation for the acute symptoms in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Excessive mediastinal effusion: Excessive mediastinal effusion can potentially cause compression of the heart and lead to symptoms similar to cardiac tamponade. However, the specific mention of raised central venous pressure and pulsus paradoxus points more towards cardiac tamponade as the likely diagnosis."}]},{"n":134,"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis for a female patient, aged 65, who visits the outpatient department with symptoms of breathlessness and fatigue? During examination, the patient's neck veins were engorged, and her jugular venous pulse displayed a swift X and Y descent.","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Restricted cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Constrictive pericarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis is a condition characterized by inflammation and fibrosis of the pericardium, the sac-like structure surrounding the heart. It leads to the stiffening of the pericardium, impairing the normal filling and functioning of the heart.Therefore, based on the given clinical features of shortness of breath, tiredness, engorged neck veins, and JVP showing rapid X and Y descent, the most likely diagnosis is constrictive pericarditis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Cardiac tamponade: Typically presents with elevated JVP, but it shows a prominent X descent and an absent Y descent (due to impaired filling), not rapid X and Y descents."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Dilated cardiomyopathy: This condition generally presents with signs of heart failure and an elevated JVP, but the JVP waveform does not typically show rapid X and Y descents."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. Restrictive cardiomyopathy: This condition can present with symptoms similar to constrictive pericarditis, but it usually has a slow Y descent in the JVP rather than a rapid one."}]},{"n":135,"q":"A female patient with fever and malar rashes was found to have vegetations on either side of the heart valves. What can be the diagnosis of the patient?","o":[{"t":"Infective endocarditis"},{"t":"Libman sacks endocarditis"},{"t":"Rheumatic Heart Disease"},{"t":"None of the above"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Libman sacks endocarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Based on the given clinical findings, the most probable diagnosis for the patient is Libman-Sacks endocarditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is a form of nonbacterial endocarditis (also known as nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis) that is commonly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). It is characterized by the presence of small, sterile vegetations on the heart valves, particularly the mitral and aortic valves."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Infective endocarditis: While infective endocarditis can also cause vegetations on the heart valves, the presence of malar rashes suggests an autoimmune condition like SLE rather than an infectious etiology."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":136,"q":"A 50 year old lady with hypertension is brought with shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion for the past week. Physical examination reveals the presence of elevated jugular venous pressure and crepitations in the lungs along with palpable liver 3 cm below the costal margin. The image of her legs is given below what is the finding called?","o":[{"t":"Superficial venous thrombosis"},{"t":"Deep vein thrombosis"},{"t":"Clubbing"},{"t":"Pitting pedal edema"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Pitting pedal edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pitting pedal edema refers to the swelling of the feet and ankles that occurs when pressure is applied to the affected area, causing an indentation or \"pit\" that persists for some time after the pressure is released. This type of edema is commonly associated with congestive cardiac failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[4614]},{"n":137,"q":"Which cardiac chamber enlargement is seen in the case of mitral stenosis on chest x-ray initially?","o":[{"t":"Left atrium"},{"t":"Right atrium"},{"t":"Left ventricle"},{"t":"Right ventricle"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Left atrium:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral stenosis leads to increased pressure in the left atrium, causing left atrial enlargement. On a chest x-ray, this can be visualized as a bulge or enlargement of the left heart border."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B, C, and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":138,"q":"Osborn waves in ECG are seen in:","o":[{"t":"Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Hypothermia"},{"t":"Hypocalcaemia"},{"t":"Hypokalaemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hypothermia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Osborn waves, also known as J waves or camel humps , are a characteristic ECG finding seen in hypothermia . They appear as positive deflections at the J point, just following the QRS complex. These waves are most prominent in the precordial leads (V2-V6) and can be seen in other leads as well."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":139,"q":"Which of the following murmur increases on standing?","o":[{"t":"HOCM"},{"t":"MR"},{"t":"MS"},{"t":"VSD"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - HOCM (Hypertrophic Obstructive Cardiomyopathy):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In HOCM, the murmur increases on standing due to decreased preload. This is known as the \"standing murmur\" phenomenon . HOCM is characterized by the hypertrophy of the left ventricular wall, particularly the septum. This hypertrophy causes obstruction to the outflow of blood from the left ventricle during systole, resulting in a murmur."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are not associated with a murmur that increases on standing."}]},{"n":140,"q":"A 40 year old male patient came to ED with complaints of weakness, paresthesia, and breathing difficulty. Relevant investigations were done. The ECG obtained is suggestive of:","o":[{"t":"Hypokalaemia"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"},{"t":"Hypocalcemia"},{"t":"Hypercalcemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypokalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This option is correct because the ECG findings in the question, such as ST segment depression, decreased amplitude of T waves, and increased amplitude of U waves, are characteristic of hypokalemia . Hypokalemia refers to low levels of potassium in the blood , which can lead to disturbances in cardiac electrical conduction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hyperkalemia: Hyperkalemia is characterized by different ECG changes, including peaked T waves, prolonged PR interval, widened QRS complex, and a sine wave pattern."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypocalcemia: Hypocalcemia can present with its own characteristic ECG changes, such as prolonged QT interval, but those findings are not mentioned in the question."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypercalcemia: Hypercalcemia can lead to various ECG changes, including short QT interval and prolonged PR interval, but these findings are not mentioned in the question."}],"qi":[4620]},{"n":141,"q":"What is grade 2 hypertension values according to the American Heart Association?","o":[{"t":">130/80 mmHg"},{"t":"‡140/90 mmHg"},{"t":">150/96 mmHg"},{"t":"‡160/100 mmHg"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - ‡140/90 mmHg :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the current guidelines of AHA, immediate pharmacological management should be initiated in patients with hypertension if their blood pressure readings are consistently at or above ‡140/90 mmHg"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":142,"q":"A 65 year old female patient is brought to the emergency department in a state of unconsciousness. Her BP is 70/50 mm of Hg. Her ECG is shown below. What is the next best step in the management of her condition?","o":[{"t":"IV verapamil"},{"t":"IV adenosine"},{"t":"Carotid massage"},{"t":"DC cardioversion"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - DC cardioversion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DC cardioversion is the correct option. In the case of a patient with atrial fibrillation who is unconscious and hemodynamically unstable, the next best step in management is immediate DC cardioversion. DC cardioversion involves delivering an electric shock synchronized with the patient's heartbeat to restore normal heart rhythm. This intervention is necessary to stabilize the patient and restore normal cardiac output."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and C are not appropriate management choices for this patient with AF with hemodynamic compromise."}],"qi":[4623]},{"n":143,"q":"For which of the following conditions is automatic implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (AICD) implantation performed?","o":[{"t":"Brugada syndrome"},{"t":"Ventricular fibrillation"},{"t":"Acute coronary syndrome with low ejection fraction"},{"t":"All the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - All the above:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Automatic implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (AICD) implantation is indicated for all of the mentioned conditions."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Brugada syndrome : AICD implantation is recommended for patients with Brugada syndrome who have a history of aborted sudden cardiac death (SCD), syncope, or documented ventricular arrhythmias."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ventricular fibrillation: AICD implantation is the treatment of choice for patients who have survived a cardiac arrest due to ventricular fibrillation (VF) or sustained ventricular tachycardia (VT) with hemodynamic compromise."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute coronary syndrome with low ejection fraction: AICD implantation is considered for patients with acute coronary syndrome who have reduced left ventricular ejection fraction (EF) (typically £35%) and are at risk of sudden cardiac death."}]},{"n":144,"q":"Who is classified as a category III non-heart-beating donor?","o":[{"t":"Dead on arrival"},{"t":"A patient who died after failed resuscitation after reaching the hospital"},{"t":"A patient who was bought dead to the hospital"},{"t":"A patient who is awaiting cardiac arrest in the hospital"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - A patient who is awaiting cardiac arrest in the hospital:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This refers to a patient who is in a critical condition and expected to die imminently within the hospital setting. These patients may have conditions such as end-stage organ failure, irreversible brain damage, or severe trauma that cannot be treated successfully. They are kept under palliative care until their natural death occurs, at which point their organs may be considered for donation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Dead on arrival: This is a category 1 donor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - A patient who died after failed resuscitation after reaching the hospital: This is a category 2 donor. In this case, resuscitation efforts were attempted upon the patient's arrival at the hospital but were unsuccessful. While this patient did experience cardiac arrest, they would not fall under the category of no-heart-beating donor as resuscitation attempts were made."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - A patient who was bought dead to the hospital: If a patient is brought dead to the hospital, it means they were already deceased upon arrival. Similar to the first option, this patient would not meet the criteria for no-heart-beating donation."}]},{"n":145,"q":"In which electrolyte abnormality is Pseudo P pulmonale observed?","o":[{"t":"Hypokalaemia"},{"t":"Hyponatremia"},{"t":"Hypocalcaemia"},{"t":"Hypercalcemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypokalemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pseudo p pulmonale is seen in hypokalemia, and it refers to tall peaked P waves seen in patients with this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":146,"q":"A 50-year-old man was brought to the emergency in an unconscious state. He had a fever for the past two days and is a known case of severe COPD. His ECG is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Ventricular tachycardia"},{"t":"Multifocal atrial tachycardia"},{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Atrial tachycardia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Multifocal atrial tachycardia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Looking at the provided ECG, several key features suggest the diagnosis of multifocal atrial tachycardia: Irregular R-R intervals: The distance between consecutive R-waves (R-R intervals) varies, indicating irregularity in the heart rate. P-waves with different morphologies: In MAT, the P-waves originate from different sites within the atria, resulting in varying shapes and configurations. Heart rate >100 beats per minute: MAT is characterized by a rapid heart rate, typically exceeding 100 beats per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregular R-R intervals: The distance between consecutive R-waves (R-R intervals) varies, indicating irregularity in the heart rate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P-waves with different morphologies: In MAT, the P-waves originate from different sites within the atria, resulting in varying shapes and configurations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart rate >100 beats per minute: MAT is characterized by a rapid heart rate, typically exceeding 100 beats per minute."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These features are consistent with the diagnosis of multifocal atrial tachycardia. MAT is a supraventricular tachycardia characterized by the presence of three or more different P-wave morphologies in the ECG, reflecting the activation of multiple ectopic atrial foci."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Irregular R-R intervals: The distance between consecutive R-waves (R-R intervals) varies, indicating irregularity in the heart rate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P-waves with different morphologies: In MAT, the P-waves originate from different sites within the atria, resulting in varying shapes and configurations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Heart rate >100 beats per minute: MAT is characterized by a rapid heart rate, typically exceeding 100 beats per minute."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4627]},{"n":147,"q":"Which of the following is used in the treatment of late cardiovascular syphilis?","o":[{"t":"Benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units weekly for three weeks"},{"t":"Benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units as single dose"},{"t":"Benzathine penicillin 12-24 million units for 21 days"},{"t":"Tetracycline 2g daily"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Benzathine penicillin 2.4 million units weekly for three weeks:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Late cardiovascular syphilis refers to the tertiary stage of syphilis where the infection has progressed and affected the cardiovascular system. It is characterized by the formation of gummas (soft, non-cancerous growths) in the cardiovascular tissues, including the heart, aorta, and blood vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The treatment of choice for late cardiovascular syphilis is benzathine penicillin. Benzathine penicillin is a long-acting form of penicillin that is administered intramuscularly. The recommended regimen for late cardiovascular syphilis is benzathine penicillin 2.4 millionunits administered once weekly for three weeks. This treatment regimen ensures adequateantibiotic levels in the blood to effectively eliminate the Treponema pallidum bacteria thatcause syphilis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":148,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a component of Syndrome Z?","o":[{"t":"HDL cholesterol <40mg/dL in men"},{"t":"Blood pressure more than 130/85 mmHg"},{"t":"Fasting triglyceride of more than 150 mg/dl"},{"t":"LDL more than 100mg/dl"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - LDL more than 100mg/dl:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Syndrome Z, also known as Metabolic Syndrome, is a cluster of risk factors that increase the risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes. It is characterized by a combination of obesity (especially abdominal obesity), insulin resistance or glucose intolerance, hypertension (blood pressure more than 130/85 mmHg), and dyslipidemia (elevated triglycerides and/or reduced HDL cholesterol)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"LDL more than 100mg/dl: This is the correct answer. While LDL (low-density lipoprotein) cholesterol is an important lipid parameter to assess cardiovascular risk, it is not specifically included as a component of metabolic syndrome. However, it is worth noting that dyslipidemia in metabolic syndrome typically involves elevated triglycerides and decreased HDL cholesterol levels rather than specific LDL cutoffs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are components of metabolic syndrome"}]},{"n":149,"q":"Two brothers were arguing over a property dispute when the elder of the two complained of chest pain and collapsed and was later declared brought dead by the hospital. His family says he was previously healthy and there was no similar disease in the family members. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Infective cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Acute myocardial infarction"},{"t":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Takotsubo cardiomyopathy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, also known as stress-induced cardiomyopathy or broken heart syndrome, is a condition characterized by sudden temporary weakening of the heart muscle."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It is typically triggered by intense emotional or physical stress. The name \"Takotsubo\" refersto the shape of the affected left ventricle, which resembles a Japanese octopus trap."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the given scenario, the sudden onset of chest pain and collapse in the elder brother during an argument over a property dispute suggests a possible emotional trigger, which is commonly associated with Takotsubo cardiomyopathy. It is important to note that emotional stressors, such as arguments or intense emotional experiences, can lead to the release of stress hormones that can have a direct impact on the heart, causing transient cardiac dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Infective cardiomyopathy: Infective cardiomyopathy refers to myocardial inflammation caused by an infection, such as viral or bacterial myocarditis. While infective cardiomyopathy can present with chest pain and sudden death, there is no mention of an infectious illness or symptoms suggestive of an infection in the given scenario."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Acute myocardial infarction: Acute myocardial infarction, commonly known as a heart attack, occurs when there is a sudden blockage of blood flow to the heart muscle, usually due to a clot in a coronary artery. While chest pain and sudden death can be associated with a heart attack, the absence of risk factors, such as atherosclerosis or a previous history of heart disease, and the presence of emotional stress as a triggering factor make acute myocardial infarction less likely in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a genetic heart condition characterized by abnormal thickening of the heart muscle, leading to impaired heart function. While hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can cause sudden cardiac death, the absence of a family history of similar diseases makes it less likely in this scenario."}]},{"n":150,"q":"'a' wave in JVP corresponds to?","o":[{"t":"Right atrial contraction"},{"t":"Closure of tricuspid valve"},{"t":"Onset of ventricular systole"},{"t":"Maximal atrial filling"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Right atrial contraction:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"'a' wave in JVP corresponds to right atrial contraction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the jugular venous pulse (JVP) waveform, there are different waves that correspond to specific events in the cardiac cycle. The 'a' wave represents the contraction of the right atrium, specifically the atrial systole."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"During the cardiac cycle, the 'a' wave occurs following the 'c' wave. The 'c' wave is caused by the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular contraction. As the ventricles contract and the tricuspid valve closes, the right atrium contracts, leading to a rise in pressure. This atrial contraction creates the 'a' wave in the JVP waveform."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":151,"q":"Relative bradycardia occurs in:","o":[{"t":"Typhoid fever"},{"t":"Q fever"},{"t":"Leptospirosis"},{"t":"All of the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - All of the above:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Relative bradycardia refers to a situation where the heart rate is lower than expected in the presence of an elevated body temperature. It is characterized by a heart rate that is relatively slower compared to the degree of fever. Relative bradycardia can occur in various infectious diseases, including typhoid fever, Q fever, and leptospirosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In typhoid fever, caused by Salmonella typhi, relative bradycardia is a classic finding. The fever is typically high but the heart rate remains disproportionately lower than expected."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"There are no incorrect choices in this question, as all of the options A, B & C (typhoid fever, Q fever, and leptospirosis) are correct"}]},{"n":152,"q":"What is the distinguishing characteristic that sets apart cardiac tamponade and tension pneumothorax?","o":[{"t":"Breath sounds"},{"t":"Muffled heart sounds"},{"t":"Increased heart rate"},{"t":"Raised JVP"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Breath sounds:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In tension pneumothorax, breath sounds are diminished or absent on the affected side. The accumulation of air compresses the lung, leading to reduced air entry and decreased breath sounds."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are associated with cardiac tamponade and tension pneumothorax."}]},{"n":153,"q":"Which electrocardiogram lead would display the highest R wave amplitude when the cardiac axis is positioned at +90 degrees?","o":[{"t":"Lead aVF"},{"t":"Lead aVL"},{"t":"Lead I"},{"t":"Lead II"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lead aVF:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cardiac axis represents the overall direction of electrical activity in the heart. The positive (+) sign indicates that the electrical activity is predominantly directed towards the positive electrode in a particular lead."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lead aVF: Lead aVF is positioned on the bottom of the hexaxial reference system, and it looks upwards towards the heart. In a normal heart with a +90-degree axis, the electrical activity is directed downwards and to the left side of the body. Therefore, lead aVF would show a maximum R wave, as it is oriented towards the direction of the electrical activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":154,"q":"A 70 year old man collapsed in his house and was rushed to the hospital. On arrival, his ECG tracing was as shown below. Which of the following is the best intervention for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Use of automated external defibrillator"},{"t":"Injection of vasopressin 40 IU"},{"t":"Injection of Adrenaline 1mg IV"},{"t":"Injection of Atropine 0.5mg IV"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Injection of Adrenaline 1mg IV:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the case of an ECG tracing showing asystole, which represents the absence of any electrical activity in the heart, the best intervention is immediate cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) followed by the administration of intravenous epinephrine (adrenaline) 1mg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Injection of Adrenaline (epinephrine) 1mg IV: This is the correct intervention for asystole. Epinephrine is a vasopressor and cardiac stimulant that helps increase coronary and cerebral blood flow during CPR, enhancing the chances of successful resuscitation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and D are not the best treatment options in this patient. The appropriate course of management would be to start CPR followed by IV adrenaline."}],"qi":[4636]},{"n":155,"q":"An intern is asked to measure the blood pressure of a patient with cardiac tamponade. He/she should ask the patient to:","o":[{"t":"Hold breath"},{"t":"Take rapid shallow breaths"},{"t":"Take long deep breaths"},{"t":"Breathe normally"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Breathe normally:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When measuring blood pressure in a patient with cardiac tamponade, it is important to ensure that the patient is in a relaxed state and breathing normally. This is because taking deep breaths or holding the breath can potentially alter the intrathoracic pressure and affect the hemodynamic status of the patient."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cardiac tamponade is a condition characterized by the accumulation of fluid or blood in the pericardial space, which puts pressure on the heart and impairs its ability to fill and pump effectively. This can lead to decreased cardiac output and compromised blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this scenario, it is crucial to measure the patient's blood pressure accurately to assess the severity of the tamponade and guide appropriate management. Asking the patient to breathe normally helps maintain stable intrathoracic pressure and provides a more accurate reflection of the patient's true blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}]},{"n":156,"q":"What is the potential risk of adding verapamil to a hypertensive patient already on metoprolol?","o":[{"t":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"t":"Bradycardia with AV block"},{"t":"Tachycardia"},{"t":"Torsades de pointes"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Bradycardia with AV block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metoprolol is a beta-blocker medication that primarily works by blocking beta-adrenergic receptors in the heart, leading to a decrease in heart rate and cardiac contractility. Verapamil, on the other hand, is a calcium channel blocker that primarily acts by blocking calcium channels in the heart and blood vessels, resulting in vasodilation and a decrease in heart rate."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When verapamil is added to metoprolol, there is an increased risk of bradycardia (slow heart rate) with AV block (impairment of electrical conduction between the atria and ventricles). This is because both medications have negative chronotropic effects, meaning they decrease the heart rate. The combination of these drugs can potentiate this effect andlead to excessive slowing of the heart rate, potentially resulting in bradycardia and AV block."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":157,"q":"HV interval in the below his bundle electrogram is for conduction:","o":[{"t":"Through His - Purkinje system"},{"t":"From AV node to bundle of His"},{"t":"Purkinje to ventricular fibers"},{"t":"From bundle of His and bundle branches to ventricles"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - From bundle of His and bundle branches to ventricles:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The correct answer for the HV interval in the given His bundle electrogram is \"From bundle of His and bundle branches to ventricles.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The HV interval represents the time it takes for electrical conduction to occur from the His bundle and bundle branches to the ventricles of the heart."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B and C are incorrect."}],"qi":[4644]},{"n":158,"q":"Which investigation is the most suitable for diagnosing myocardial infarction (MI) within a timeframe of 2-4 hours, as reported by a patient with chest pain?","o":[{"t":"LDH"},{"t":"CKMB"},{"t":"Troponin-T"},{"t":"BNP"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Troponin-T:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Troponin-T is a cardiac biomarker that is highly specific to cardiac muscle injury. It is released into the bloodstream when there is damage to the heart muscle, such as in myocardial infarction (MI). Troponin-T levels typically start to rise within 3-4 hours after the onset of myocardial injury and remain elevated for several days. Therefore, it is considered the best investigation for diagnosing MI within 2-4 hours of symptom onset."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Troponin-T measurement is widely used in clinical practice for the diagnosis of MI, as it provides high sensitivity and specificity for cardiac injury. Elevated troponin-T levels, in conjunction with clinical symptoms and other diagnostic tests, help confirm the diagnosis of MI and guide appropriate management."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - LDH (Lactate dehydrogenase): LDH is an enzyme found in various tissues, including the heart. However, it lacks specificity for cardiac injury. LDH levels may increase in MI, but it is not the best investigation for early diagnosis within 2-4 hours."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - CK-MB (Creatine kinase-MB): CK-MB is an isoform of the enzyme creatine kinase that is predominantly found in the heart. It is released into the bloodstream following cardiac muscle injury. However, CK-MB levels may take several hours to rise, and it is not as specific for early diagnosis of MI as troponin-T."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide): BNP is a hormone released by the ventricles of the heart in response to increased stretching of cardiac muscle cells. It is primarily used for diagnosing and assessing the severity of heart failure. BNP levels are not specific to myocardial infarction and do not provide timely diagnosis within 2-4 hours."}]},{"n":159,"q":"A 56-year-old unresponsive male was brought to the ER with GCS less than 7 and ECG shows ventricular fibrillation. All are true about steps followed in the management of ventricular fibrillation except?","o":[{"t":"CPR cycle duration is 2 minutes"},{"t":"Chest compressions at 100-120/min"},{"t":"Start CPR followed by immediate defibrillation"},{"t":"Rescue breaths 15:2"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Rescue breaths 15:2:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rescue breaths are given in the ratio of 30:2 not 15:2"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore this option is the answer"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - CPR cycle duration is 2 minutes: This is true and represents the recommended duration for each cycle of CPR (i.e., a combination of chest compressions and rescue breaths) before reassessment of the patient's rhythm and pulse."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Chest compressions at 100-120/min: This is true and represents the recommended rate of chest compressions during CPR. Adequate chest compressions help maintain blood circulation and improve the chances of successful defibrillation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Start CPR followed by immediate defibrillation: This is true and represents the standard approach to managing ventricular fibrillation. In the event of a cardiac arrest due to ventricular fibrillation, immediate CPR (consisting of chest compressions and rescue breaths) should be initiated, and defibrillation should be performed as soon as a defibrillator is available. Early defibrillation is crucial in restoring a normal heart rhythm."}]},{"n":160,"q":"In a 55-year-old male who arrives at the emergency department, symptoms of chest pain, sweating, nausea, and difficulty breathing have been experienced for the past four hours. The patient has a smoking history of one pack per day for 20 years and no previous medical conditions. Upon examination, the patient's pulse is recorded at 76 beats per minute, and blood pressure measures 110/70 mmHg. An electrocardiogram (ECG) has been performed and is provided below. Based on the information provided, what is the most probable diagnosis for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Prinzmetal angina"},{"t":"Myocardial ischemia"},{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Myocardial ischemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the ECG given above, the ST segment elevation is present in the: Anterior leads (V2-V4) Lateral leads (V5-V6, I, and aVL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior leads (V2-V4)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lateral leads (V5-V6, I, and aVL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient’s symptoms and ECG findings are suggestive of myocardial infarction in the anterior and lateral walls of the heart."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most likely cause of it is an occlusion in the proximal left anterior descending artery."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior leads (V2-V4)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lateral leads (V5-V6, I, and aVL)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect."}],"qi":[4648]},{"n":161,"q":"Which of the following medications does not decrease mortality in a 70-year-old male patient diagnosed with heart failure and an ejection fraction","o":[{"t":"Metoprolol"},{"t":"Digoxin"},{"t":"Captopril"},{"t":"K+ sparing diuretics"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Digoxin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digoxin is primarily used to control symptoms and improve quality of life in patients with heart failure. It helps in reducing heart rate and controlling certain arrhythmias associated with heart failure, but it has not been shown to reduce mortality."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Metoprolol: Metoprolol is a beta-blocker and has been shown to reduce mortality in patients with heart failure. It improves symptoms, reduces hospitalizations, and improves overall survival."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Captopril: Captopril is an ACE inhibitor and has been demonstrated to reduce mortality and improve outcomes in patients with heart failure. It helps in reducing afterload, improving cardiac function, and slowing disease progression."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - K+ sparing diuretics: K+ sparing diuretics such as spironolactone and eplerenone are used in combination with other heart failure medications. They help in reducing fluid retention and edema but have also been shown to reduce mortality in patients with heart failure."}]},{"n":162,"q":"Which of the following leads to a continuous murmur?","o":[{"t":"Peripheral Pulmonic stenosis"},{"t":"Severe Pulmonary artery hypertension"},{"t":"Type A aortic dissection"},{"t":"Rupture of cardiac chamber"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Peripheral Pulmonic stenosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Continuous murmurs begins in systole, peaks near the second heart sound, and continues into all or part of diastole."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is seen in Peripheral pulmonic stenosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peripheral pulmonic stenosis is a Congenital heart defect"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Narrowing of one or more branches of the pulmonary artery is known as Peripheral pulmonic stenosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Due to narrowing there will be turbulence which will be present in both systole and diastole"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are not associated with a continuous murmur."}]},{"n":163,"q":"Which is true about Postural Hypotension?","o":[{"t":"Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 6 mins of postural change"},{"t":"Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 3 mins of postural change"},{"t":"Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 6 mins of postural change"},{"t":"Decrease in diastolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 3 mins of postural change"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 3 mins of postural change:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"\"Decrease in systolic blood pressure 20 mm Hg within 3 mins of postural change.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Postural hypotension , also known as orthostatic hypotension , refers to a drop in blood pressure that occurs upon assuming an upright position. It is characterized by a decrease in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mm Hg or a decrease in diastolic blood pressure of at least 10 mm Hg within 3 minutes of standing up."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C and D are incorrect"}]},{"n":164,"q":"What is the most accurate and distinctive marker for Myocardial Infarction (MI)?","o":[{"t":"Troponin"},{"t":"Cytokeratin"},{"t":"Myoglobin"},{"t":"CPK- MM"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct options: A"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Troponin is the preferred and most widely used biomarker for diagnosing myocardial infarction. It is highly specific to cardiac muscle and has a high sensitivity for detecting cardiac injury. When there is damage to the heart muscle, troponin is released into the bloodstream , and elevated levels can be measured through blood tests."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":165,"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the modified Duke’s criteria?","o":[{"t":"Single positive blood culture for HACEK group is a major criterion"},{"t":"Single positive blood culture for Coxiella burnetti is a major criterion"},{"t":"Complete dehiscence of prosthetic valve"},{"t":"Documentation of four minor criteria allows a clinical diagnosis"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Single positive blood culture for Coxiella burnetti is a major criterion:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A single positive blood culture for Coxiella burnetii is a major criterion in the modified Duke's criteria for infective endocarditis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Single positive blood culture for HACEK group is a major criterion: A single positive blood culture for HACEK group organisms (Haemophilus species, Aggregatibacter actinomycetemcomitans, Cardiobacterium hominis, Eikenella corrodens, and Kingella) is not considered a major criterion in the modified Duke's criteria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Complete dehiscence of prosthetic valve: Complete dehiscence of a prosthetic valve is not a criterion in the modified Duke's criteria."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Documentation of four minor criteria allows a clinical diagnosis: A definitive diagnosis of endocarditis can be made in patients with two major criteria, one major and three minor criteria, or five minor criteria."}]},{"n":166,"q":"A person collapses following RTA and you start CPR. The rescue team has arrived. Which of the following is correct among the given options?","o":[{"t":"Continue 5 rounds of CPR @ 30:2 followed by application of AED"},{"t":"Continue CPR while the team applies AED paddies"},{"t":"Stop CPR and let them apply paddies"},{"t":"Handover the patient and leave"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Continue CPR while the team applies AED paddies:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the above scenario, the recommended action is to continue performing CPR while the rescue team applies the AED paddles."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is because CPR provides oxygenated blood to the victim's vital organs, including the brain and heart, while the AED can deliver a shock to the heart to restore its natural rhythm."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Continue 5 rounds of CPR @ 30:2 followed by application of AED: \" Continue 5 rounds of CPR @ 30:2\" followed by application of AED is not recommended because it suggests stopping CPR after five cycles and applying the AED. Instead, it is advisable to continue CPR until the AED is fully operational."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Stop CPR and let them apply paddles: \"Stop CPR and let them apply paddles\" is also not recommended because it can lead to a drop in blood flow to the vital organs, which can cause irreversible damage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Handover the patient and leave: “Handover the patient and leave” is not recommended because the responder has a duty of care towards the victim until the arrival of the rescue team. Leaving the scene without handing over the patient to the rescue team can be considered abandonment and can lead to legal consequences."}]},{"n":167,"q":"A Post-operative immobile patient develops sudden onset severe calf pain. The best investigation to be done is?","o":[{"t":"CTPA"},{"t":"Duplex USG"},{"t":"X-ray"},{"t":"D-dimer assay"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Duplex USG:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Deep vein thrombosis in the lower extremities usually begins in the calf and propagates proximally to the popliteal vein, femoral vein, and iliac vein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common symptom is a cramp or “charley horse” in the lower calf that persists and intensifies over several days."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"With acute DVT, the vein loses its compressibility because of passive distention by acute thrombus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duplex USG is the investigation of choice for DVT."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It relies on the loss of vein compressibility as the primary diagnostic criterion."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Venous flow dynamics can be examined with Doppler imaging."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CT and MRI are reserved for patients with technically poor duplex USG."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - CTPA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Invasive catheter-based tests are reserved for patients with unsatisfactory chest CT or if catheter-directed thrombolysis is planned."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - X-ray:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray is used in the diagnosis of pulmonary embolism, where Westermark’s sign, Palla sign, and Hampton’s hump could be observed."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - D-dimer assay:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"D-dimer is not specific and less sensitive for DVT than PE as the thrombus of DVT is smaller."}]},{"n":168,"q":"A 60-year-old morbidly obese patient develops respiratory distress on the third postoperative day after a major surgery. The first differential diagnosis is?","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Air embolism"},{"t":"Atelectasis"},{"t":"Pneumothorax"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Pulmonary embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The onset of respiratory distress on the third postoperative day after a major surgery indicated Pulmonary thromboembolism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A morbidly obese elderly patient is a risk factor for DVT, meaning a clot could break from the leg veins that could travel to the heart and cause pulmonary thromboembolism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prevention: Patients with pulmonary embolism must be administered Low Molecular Weight Heparin or Enoxaparin postoperatively in any person who underwent cancer surgery or any other surgeries"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Air embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Air embolism presents with tachypnea, and cyanosis along with neurological features like altered mental status, and seizures."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Atelectasis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atelectasis occurs so commonly in the perioperative period, this form is also called perioperative respiratory failure"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pneumothorax:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pneumothorax presents with dyspnea/chest pain/anxiety along with other features such as severe hypotension, central cyanosis, etc.,"}]},{"n":169,"q":"A 70 year old male patient presents to the OPD with multiple, recurrent fainting episodes. An ECG was taken and it shows P waves at a rate of 75 bpm and qrs complexes at a rate of 35 bpm. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Mobitz type II heart block"},{"t":"Stokes adams syndrome"},{"t":"First degree heart block"},{"t":"Wolf parkinson white syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B- Stokes adams syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this case, the ECG shows P waves at 75 bpm which is normal, but the qrs complex has a rate of 35 bpm indicating bradycardia. Both the waveforms having different rates indicate a complete heart block."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stokes Adams syndrome is a condition characterized by multiple fainting episodes due to complete heart block. It is mainly diagnosed through ECG and the definitive treatment options include insertion of a permanent cardiac pacemaker."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A- Mobitz type II heart block: Mobitz type II heart block is a 2nd degree heart block in which there are one or more non-conducted p waves. It is not a complete heart block."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C-First degree heart block: First degree heart block is characterized by prolonged PR interval. It is asymptomatic."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D-Wolf parkinson white syndrome: Wolf parkinson white syndrome is characterized by the presence of an accessory bundle of kent through which the electrical conduction bypasses AV node. It does not cause a complete heart block."}]},{"n":170,"q":"A patient came with the complains of fatigue and difficulty breathing, On examination, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds were found. X-ray is shown below. Identify the disease.","o":[{"t":"Acute pericarditis"},{"t":"Bronchial asthma"},{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Atrial myxoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Constructive pericarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The X Ray image is suggestive of Constrictive pericarditis and the clinical features are also consistent with Constrictive pericarditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a condition in which granulation tissue formation in the pericardium results in loss of pericardial elasticity leading to restriction in the ventricular filling"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray appearance: Egg in cup appearance"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IOC: CT scan"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Acute pericarditis: A flask shaped, enlarged cardiac silhouette will be observed in this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Bronchial asthma : This can present as pulmonary hyperinflation, bronchial wall thickening and peribronchial cuffing."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Atrial myxoma: This can present with cardiomegaly, left atrial enlargement, vascular redistribution, prominent pulmonary trunk."}],"qi":[4653]},{"n":171,"q":"Which medication should be included in the treatment regimen of a patient with a history of atherosclerosis who recently underwent a successful circumflex artery bypass and is currently taking lisinopril, verapamil, and metoprolol?","o":[{"t":"PDE 3 inhibitor"},{"t":"Direct oral anticoagulant"},{"t":"P2Y12 receptor blocker"},{"t":"PDE 5 inhibitor"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: P2Y12 receptor blocker"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P2Y12 receptor blockers, such as clopidogrel, ticagrelor, and prasugrel, are antiplatelet medications commonly used in patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), including the placement of stents."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These drugs inhibit platelet activation and aggregation, reducing the risk of clot formation in the stent and preventing complications such as stent thrombosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A. PDE 3 inhibitor: Phosphodiesterase-3 (PDE 3) inhibitors are commonly used in the treatment of heart failure and peripheral vascular disease. Option B. Direct oral anticoagulant: Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs), may be considered in some patients with certain indications, such as atrial fibrillation or mechanical heart valves, the routine use of DOACs may not be necessary in all patients who undergo a circumflex artery bypass for atherosclerosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. PDE 5 inhibitor: Phosphodiesterase-5 (PDE 5) inhibitors, such as sildenafil, tadalafil, and vardenafil, are primarily used for the treatment of erectile dysfunction and pulmonary arterial hypertension."}]},{"n":172,"q":"Match the following diagnostic criteria with their diseases. 1. Beckett-Jones criteria a. Bronchial asthma. 2. Sevitt's Clinical Criteria b. Pulmonary tuberculosis. 3. Sherwood-Jones criteria c. Rheumatic fever. 4. Kenneth-Jones criteria d. Fat embolism\n1. Beckett-Jones criteria a. Bronchial asthma.\n2. Sevitt's Clinical Criteria b. Pulmonary tuberculosis.\n3. Sherwood-Jones criteria c. Rheumatic fever.\n4. Kenneth-Jones criteria d. Fat embolism","o":[{"t":"1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b"},{"t":"1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d"},{"t":"1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b"},{"t":"1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Beckett-Jones criteria c. Rheumatic fever"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Sevitt's Clinical Criteria d. Fat embolism"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Sherwood-Jones criteria a. Bronchial asthma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. Kenneth-Jones criteria b. Pulmonary tuberculosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: 1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: 1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: 1-c, 2-a, 3-b, 4-d"}]},{"n":173,"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis for a 65-year-old woman who arrives at the outpatient department with symptoms of breathlessness and fatigue? She has no previous medical history of reactive airway disease or heart disease, but she has undergone breast cancer treatment including breast conservation surgery and chemoradiation. On examination, engorged neck veins and jugular venous pressure (JVP) indicate a rapid x and y descent. The chest x-ray findings are as follows.","o":[{"t":"Constrictive pericarditis"},{"t":"Cardiac tamponade"},{"t":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},{"t":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Constrictive pericarditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constrictive pericarditis is a condition characterized by the inflammation and fibrosis of the pericardium, the sac-like membrane surrounding the heart. This fibrotic thickening of the pericardium restricts the ability of the heart to expand and fill properly during diastole, leading to impaired cardiac function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The engorged neck veins and rapid x and y descent in JVP are classic features seen in constrictive pericarditis due to pericardial shudder, an auscultatory finding associated with constrictive pericarditis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The x-ray shows signs of pericardial calcification or thickening, which are characteristic features of this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cardiac tamponade: Cardiac tamponade is a condition characterized by steep ‘x’ and absent ‘y’ descent."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Dilated cardiomyopathy: Dilated cardiomyopathy is characterized by enlargement of the heart chambers and the characteristic sign of dilated cardiomyopathy is dicrotic pulse."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Restricted cardiomyopathy: Restrictive cardiomyopathy is a condition characterized by normal ventricles, but enlarged atria, and amyloid deposition in the myocardium leading to stiffness of the muscle and less compliance."}],"qi":[4660]},{"n":174,"q":"A 62 yr old man came to the hospital with a complaint of recurrent fainting episodes. His ECG shows P waves at 75/min, and QRS complexes duration is normal at 35 beats/min. The clinical diagnosis is?","o":[{"t":"2 nd degree AV block"},{"t":"Atrial tachycardia"},{"t":"First-degree AV block"},{"t":"Stokes adams syndrome"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Stokes adams syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Recurrent syncopal episodes and an unequal number of P waves and QRS complexes are features of a patient with Stokes Adam syndrome/3rd heart block/Complete heart block."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is due to a lack of coordination between the atria and ventricles leading to AV dissociation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ECG features: Prolonged PR interval Broad QRS complex The number of P waves will not be equal to the number of R waves P-P interval (atrial rate) will not match the R-R interval (ventricular rate)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Broad QRS complex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The number of P waves will not be equal to the number of R waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P-P interval (atrial rate) will not match the R-R interval (ventricular rate)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolonged PR interval"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Broad QRS complex"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The number of P waves will not be equal to the number of R waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"P-P interval (atrial rate) will not match the R-R interval (ventricular rate)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - 2 nd degree AV block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 nd -degree AV block presents with dropped beats or missed beats."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Atrial tachycardia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Atrial tachycardia is ruled out as the heart is not more than 100 beats/min."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - First-degree AV block:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"First-degree heart block shows PR prolongation of more than 200 milliseconds"}]},{"n":175,"q":"A Patient has muffled heart sounds with low blood pressure. Correct JVP finding is?","o":[{"t":"Steep x descent"},{"t":"Absent y descent"},{"t":"Steep y descent"},{"t":"Blunted x descent"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Absent y descent:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muffled heart sounds and low blood pressure is seen in cardiac tamponade."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Absent y descent and non-pulsatile elevated JVP are the JVP findings seen in cardiac tamponade due to ventricular filling defect."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Becks triad is a presenting feature of cardiac tamponade: Muffled heart sounds Hypotension Elevated JVP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muffled heart sounds"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated JVP"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Muffled heart sounds"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated JVP"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Steep x descent:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Steep x descent is seen in constrictive pericarditis along with steep y descent"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Steep y descent:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Steep y descent is seen in exaggerated tricuspid regurgitation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Blunted x descent :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Blunted x descent is seen in restrictive cardiomyopathy"}]},{"n":176,"q":"A young male patient presents with sudden onset chest pain for one hour with ECG showing STE in lead 2,3 and aVf. Which is the best treatment for this case of inferior wall MI in a multispeciality hospital?","o":[{"t":"Alteplase"},{"t":"Streptokinase"},{"t":"Primary angioplasty"},{"t":"Dabigatran"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Primary angioplasty:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Primary angioplasty/Baloon angioplasty/Percutaneous coronary intervention is the treatment of choice in patients with ST-elevation MI."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is performed within the first medical contact-to-device time, which is 90 mins."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A transradial or transfemoral approach (preferred) can be used."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Earlier bare metal stents were used, but now drug-eluting stents coated with Everolimus or Zotarolimus are used."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Alteplase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alteplase (Tissue plasminogen activator) is preferred in non–PCI–capable hospitals."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Door-in and door-out (DIDO) to initiate fibrinolysis within 30 minutes"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Streptokinase:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Streptokinase is not the treatment of choice according to the latest guidelines and it has the risk of anaphylaxis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Dabigatran:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dabigatran is not used in the treatment of MI."}]},{"n":177,"q":"A 55-year-old lady presents with complaints of dizziness and palpitations. ECG was done and is shown below. What is the best treatment?","o":[{"t":"Adenosine"},{"t":"Amiodarone"},{"t":"Beta-blocker"},{"t":"Valsalva maneuver"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Adenosine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above ECG shows narrow QRS tachycardia indicating paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The best treatment for PSVT is Inj. adenosine 6 mg/12 mg."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is administered in patients with stable systolic BP >90 mmHg"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Amiodarone: Amiodarone is used along with beta-blockers in recurrent ventricular tachycardias"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Beta-blocker: Beta-blockers may be helpful but can cause hypotension."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Valsalva maneuver: Valsalva maneuver is the initial treatment in PSVT with systolic BP >90 mmHg."}],"qi":[4665]},{"n":178,"q":"A 52-year-old lady develops sudden onset facial deviation with slurring of speech and inability to raise her right arm. The symptoms spontaneously begin to reduce and get completely resolved over the next 48 hours. She has a mid-diastolic murmur on auscultation in the left side of the heart and an irregular heart rhythm. What is the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"T.I.A"},{"t":"Thrombo-embolic stroke"},{"t":"Cardio embolic stroke"},{"t":"Paradoxical embolism"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - T.I.A (Transient ischemic attack):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The transient ischemic attack is caused by a thrombus occluding a blood vessel causing neurological manifestations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this patient, mid-diastolic murmur indicates mitral stenosis leading to atrial fibrillation which causes clot formation leading to cardio-embolic stroke."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Improvement of symptoms occurs within 1 hour and complete resolution of symptoms occurs in <24 hours due to spontaneous destruction of clot and revascularization of blood vessels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Protein C, Protein S, and Anti Thrombin III are naturally occurring anti-coagulants that destroy the clot."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Thrombo-embolic stroke:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The neurological symptoms resolved completely which indicates TIA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cardio embolic stroke:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mid-diastolic murmur and an irregular heart rhythm along with neurological symptoms cause cardio-embolic stroke, but in this case, the symptoms resolved themselves completely."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Paradoxical embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The neurological symptoms resolve completely which indicates TIA"}]},{"n":179,"q":"A lady with mitral stenosis develops sudden onset respiratory difficulty, limb edema, and pain in the calves. What is the clinical diagnosis in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"t":"Pulmonary edema"},{"t":"Cerebral embolism"},{"t":"Paradoxical embolism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Pulmonary edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral stenosis with sudden onset breathing difficulty, limb edema, and pain in calves indicates pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mitral stenosis over time causes LA dilatation which causes pulmonary venous hypertension leading to pulmonary edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary edema leads to right ventricular hypertrophy causing limb edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary edema occurs mainly due to the increased pressure in LA and the pulmonary venous circuit."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pulmonary embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pulmonary embolism causes pulmonary oligemia that does not cause cardiogenic pulmonary edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cerebral embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral embolism is ruled out as no neurological features are present in this patient."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Paradoxical embolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Paradoxical embolism is ruled as there are no neurological features present."}]},{"n":180,"q":"All of the following conditions will have an elevated JVP that rises with deep inspiration except?","o":[{"t":"HOCM"},{"t":"Constructive pericarditis"},{"t":"Cor pulmonale"},{"t":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - HOCM:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normally JVP falls on deep inspiration, but if it rises it is called the Kussmaul sign."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The Kussumaul sign is not seen in HOCM."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C, and D: (Constructive pericarditis, Cor pulmonale & Restrictive cardiomyopathy)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kussumaul sign is seen in all these conditions"}]}]);
